      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>PRACTICAL POULTRY FARMING. --- (By Redcap.) POULTRY FOR EXHIBITION.</head>
        <p>PRACTICAL POULTRY FARMING. --- (By Redcap.) POULTRY FOR EXHIBITION.</p>
        <p>((one ;nleed). WIRT TO arter.D FUR.</p>
        <p>Whatever breed or variety the amateur takes up he should know exactly what t., breed for. I mean to nay, that he should understand what the chief show characteristics of the breeds are. Some breeds are noted for their line plumage more sr than type and vice-versa. Heed T oints in some are of the chief exhibition value, whilst size alone seems to catch the judges eye with cm tai:l breeds and varieties. Before I detail these assertions let moose, that the chief aim should be all-round quality. I am afraid that in many instances such is mm t the case. A good many of our judges are influenced by abnormities, and often enough useful chow specimens salter from abnegation of the true ideal.</p>
        <p>Perfect head points count for much i.a Minoreas,and Leghorns, however good a Minnie. or Leghorn may be in colour and type—if deficit rtt in head points— it would stand little or no chance in the show p n. Therefore, once you have perfected these it is some- what easy to imp. ove the type and colour. In these two twee&amp; you should aim at ',wading specimens with • good comb and lobts. The lobes • the white appendages-- are seen on both ides of the I ird's face. 'The comb should be firmly art on and evenly serrated, whilst the lobes eltould be almond alt pod and pure in colt or, providing the latter are of good substance and welbanaped they reed not be abnormal in size. The number If ecrob serrations vary, and so long.. they are well-shaped (thus A) it matters little, the avertge, howerer, is abost fire. 'I here in a commoa defect especially with Black Minorca. known 1%, white in face. This is te, able with strains that have Spanish blood inf. The face of the black Spanish is practically opal " all lobe," ar.d if great care In n t used in lle selection of steck I irds) it C3tlieS br E'er. of the :!finorrO I ot of tro.Ne, Meek Nlinorca zon•-a- I days is u-ualle panned by the judge which shows white in fare. I might say in panning, that there are no many good birds in he bad now, that you teed never run any risks by the infusion of Spanish blood. What has been said in favour of head points in the Minorca can also be said when speaking of the Leghorn. The only differerce is eye colour. The Minorca's eye allculd be dark while that of the Leghorn should be red or orange. We now come to</p>
        <p>It is almost difficult to speak of a fixed type in these Med iter ranean breeds: because like the weather, It seems to change often. The Leghorn BR well as the Minorca of 20 years ego is not the Leghorn and Minorra f today. The old type was m re " cobby " and " squirrel tailed 'then the C leggy and gamey type of to-day. The present-day type though is conch smarter than the old, but more difficult to obtain. The body should be erect, flat hark and a whip tail. the legs should be straight with toes well spread and not crooked. Yon :should discard npecimens with , what 14 known RR 'coached back:This fault in observed , in flowla when the b,ck is round imitend of being slalom Hat. The old fashioned squirrel tail thnuld also be avoided. The tail ahruld fall smarty away from the body in whip fashion and with not too parch sickle feathering. Tie general contour r f the Carl ahould be well arisen "end somewhat tight in feather. The foregoing remarks apply to the male bird and the eAre can be slid in regard t the hen as In type of body, the comb, however. in the females, ehould be large, fine. in texture, well serrated, but overhanging on one side cf the face.</p>
        <p>IT good a specimen you my breed, it would be useless if not mown in Rood conditi,n. The comb and wattles should be red, the legs free from scale, and feathers glossy. When getting a bird into show condition you should keep it separate from the rest of the st. cit. This should be done by housing it in a cocken! pen (a small two c-inpartment house and run), and more it about daily on to fresh ground. Liberal fteding En wholesome food with plenty ' of lean meat soon brings them into gool show condition. When thoroughly fit tin y should then go under a period of training. If a Lied is not thoroughly trained when shown, it standi little chance of winning. It is impossible for a judge to do ' jus•be if they fly about the show pen in • frightened state. Good birds have often been passed for this rely reason. Very well then. You elmuld erect a show pun in a warm r nra and place your bird in it and give constant attention. You should so place the pen that it will be often pss-ed, by no doing the occupant will soon get used to being approached. A good plan in taming them is by feeding through the wire from the tap of the pen with pieces of meat and other tit-bits. This not only tamee them but gets them used to standing errct and thus showing them- selves to advantage. A well tainted specimen alweys pleases line judge and stands• better chance of winning than untrained ones. Always remember that the time slimed to a judge is generally speaking very limited, and he cannot be exuemed t tilerate any misgivings on the part of unruly exhibits.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>OTHFM BREEDS,</head>
        <p>OTHFM BREEDS,</p>
        <p>But we must get back to other bteeds Olen the Meditertaneane, a ith these as I have shown it is clarify type, parity of colour and head points "hick count. Now with most of the other sectious of poultry it in ip netally size, feather markings, and quality of feather which crnnt. For instance! Feather markings are the chief show points in such breeda as the Pencilled Hamburgs, Rtdcape, Silver and Gold Wyandotte', Campices, limed Rocks, Silver Grey and Dark Dorking., Game (Indian and Old English); whilst size and quality of feather pre- dominate in btreds like the Brahma. Cochin, Buff, White and Block Orpingtoos, White and Buff Rocks, White and Black Wyandottes land other self coloured varieties). Now all throe self coloured vorietiro can be produced by ainale mating, but most of the other variefes are predated by a system of</p>
        <p>1...t1t1.4. MATING, This means sunning two pens ; one mated to pro- dare cockerels and the other pullets. It would be unwise for an amateur to commence breeding opera- tions with such varieties without first anderstanding the system of double mating. It is difficult for me to show you clearly by writing whet in meant by this</p>
        <p>system. flowerer, if you follow cut the advice given sea peeriou• column you would not ao far wrong. A reliable breeder would willingly demonetrats to you the value of double mating. In this direction you would in ,rn more in live minutes than by ea hour's cording. Some day I hope I may have an opportunity of giving my readers some practical demonstrations. A great deal has been written far and against the proi ens of inbreeding, and I think that the whole question is volved both for and against in the following statement. Inbreeding is absolutely esseatial when aiming at fixity of type and colour,but on the other land it is undoubtedly detrimental when practiced sills utility stock. Now seeing that we are for the present dealing with exhibition breediftp, we must deal with the question of inbreeding or show purprsee only. Sfany a strain hoe been rained by the haphazard infusion ci ' alien ' blood. The watchword which ahoultLever guide the tomb or is this -stick to one siram and one strain alone. The system to be followed is after the first 'ear. reenits -mate the best tulleis back to the cockerel which leaded the original pen.' then select the best cockerel you breed and mate him with hens of the original pen. If ru have been fortunate enough to breed a good number you !night mate a pen of brothere and sisters, the progeny from these would be invaluable for otos...mating the season following. You can thus combine to inbreed for quite a long time. When experier cc proves and demands a change of blood, always ohm am such from the original owner 01 ,0110 strain. Don't be tempted to go outside your strain for a change of blood. Sometimes you may succeed but nine times cut of ten it would prove disaettons.</p>
