      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</head>
        <p>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</p>
        <p>By Philip Snowden. M.P. THE NEW MINES BILL</p>
        <p>The Coal Mines Bill which is now before the House of Commons is very largely a bringing together of the existing statutes regulating the working of coal mines, and in addition it proposes to make a number of amendments to the existing law. It is difficult for an outsider to estimate the value of the new provisions contained in the Bill, and on this point practical miners themselves differ very much as to the value and sufficiency of the new proposals. It is proposed in this article to set out, without comment, some of the principal changes in the law which is pro- posed in this Bill. The Bill repeals the Act which was passed last year making it com- pulsory to have about the mines applicances for use in rescue work. It incorporates these provisions, with certain additional powers to be taken by the Home Secretary for making regulations in regard to rescue work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>FIREMEN AND DEPUTIES.</head>
        <p>FIREMEN AND DEPUTIES.</p>
        <p>It is proposed that in future every mine shall be under the responsible management of a single manager, and this manager must possess a first-class mine managers' certifi- cate. The Home Secretary takes power under this Bill to limit the number of mines which may be under the control of the one manager. An alteration is made in regard to firemen and deputies, such persons in future being required to devote their whole time to the work, where practicable,and the district of a mine under the charge of a fire- man or deputy shall not be so large as to prevent him from carrying out his duties in a thorough manner. There are also more stringent regulations proposed as to the qualifications for firemen and deputies. He must not be under 25 years of age,must have had at least five years' experience under- ground in a mine, and must have obtained a certificate from a mining school that he can make accurate tests for inflammable gas, and calculate the amount of air in the air current. A more strict eyesight test is also proposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>WORKING INSPECTORS.</head>
        <p>WORKING INSPECTORS.</p>
        <p>The provision in the existing law which allows the workmen to appoint, at their own cost, two of their number or any two persons, not being mining engineers, to inspect the mine, is strengthened somewhat. 'lt is now proposed that these workmen inspectors shall be allowed to go where an accident has happened to make such inspec- tion as may be necessary for ascertaining the cause of the accident. It is still proposed that these inspections by the workmen shall be made in the presence of the owner or manager. The Bill proposes some new pro- visions in regard to the ventilation of mines, and also that every part of a mine in which ten or more persons are employed at the same time shall be provided with at least two ways affording means of egress to the surface, and so arranged that in the event of either becoming impassable at any point the other will afford means of egress to the surface.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>Rau &amp;Miles,</head>
        <p>Rau &amp;Miles,</p>
        <p>Teleptrarns. "FURNISHING::</p>
        <p>THE KANGAROO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>PROHIBITING TOBACCO.</head>
        <p>PROHIBITING TOBACCO.</p>
        <p>There are a number of new prosposals in regard to provisions as to safety, and , regulations are to be framed for the classification of mines according to the amount of inflammable gas in the main, return airway. The regulations in regard to the use of n...ked lights are made more stringent. The existing prohibition of the possession of matches is strengthened by a proposal that a miner shall not be allowed to have any tobacco in his possession. The manager of a mine is given powers to search, or to have searched, all persons employed underground, but the system of inspection must be approved by the inspector of the division. In certain cases the inspector may insist upon the provision of separate roads for travelling and for haulage. There are a large number of new proposals in regard to haulage. The existing regulations in regard to roofs and sides are to be considerably strengthened. The system of signalling in mines is to be improved. The system is to be uniform, and there is to be telephonic communication between all parts of the mine in such manner as may be fixed by regula- tions. As long as persons are in the mine there shall always be at the top of every shaft competent persons to receive the signals and to transmit them. COAL DUST AND ELECTRICITY.</p>
        <p>The dangers of coal mines have been increased by the use of electricity, and it is proposed to enact that electricity shall not be used in any part of a mine where on account of the risk of explosion of gas or coal dust its use would be dangerous to life. The inspector may call upon the owner of the, mine to desist from using electricity, but if the owner refuses to do so the matter wgl have to be settled by a referee. In regard toi coal dust it is proposed that the tubs shall be so constructed and maintained as to pre- vent, as far as possible, coal dust from escaping through the sides or floors of the tubs. The roads are to be examined daily, and a report made as to their condition as to coal dust and on what steps are being taken to mitigate danger arising therefrom. COMPULSORY CLEANLINESS.</p>
        <p>One of the most revolutionary of the new provisions in this Bill is that which provides that in every mine there shall be sufficient accommodation and facilities for taking baths and drying clothes for the persons employed underground in the mines, regard being had to the number of persons so employed. Where such accommodation and facilities have been provided, the use thereof shall be obligatory on the persons employed under- ground in the mine, and every such person shall be liable to contribute one penny a week towards the expenses of maintenance, includ- ing interest on any capital expenditure, and the owner is to be entitled to deduct this penny a week from the wages of the miner. If aa owner fails to provide these baths and • drying rooms he shall be guilty of an offence under the Act, and any workman who fails to make use of them will be similarly liable. This provision will, no doubt, be hotly con- tested in Committee on account of the cost to the owners, but it will be one of the most valuable bits of grandmotherly legislation ever enacted if it can be carried. The relief, lit will be to the collier's home and the l ' collier's wife cannot be described.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CLUB.</p>
        <p>80,.r.v Coll PKTITION.—TIIO (111111ifylog toncd wit played on Satardey with the folfwelog rewalts</p>
        <p>. .. FIRST DI VISION. SKOONO DIVIbIt N. J M Nlorrey. . 3 down 0 Owen 2 down W Greenhouse 3 , J Falroloogh ... 4 „ 3 Heyberd F Pendlebury I. Widen 4 „ E A IN hits J W Bibby 0 Hutchings CO Stone 6 , A WerLumo ... 3 „ TB Ilsndley</p>
        <p>The I oral Government Board In a report just hoed. warns tho public ugoire,t [hiking Powders and self•raieing flours containing to racer. of Calcium l'hoephate and Solphat• of Lime, the me a whir h I. denorcus to health. It sires us plmeure, therefore, to draw our readers' attention to the fact that Hamrick', Baking Powder Is elaolutely free from these and all other , iropuritim, art that, moreover, it cc ntains tho onset and rpproved constituents h. the enact proportion, which form a perfect Baking Powder. Them la no want, in tering Itotwick'e Baking Powder, al every portion of it contains a ronximum of miring power in the most concentrated form. We would warn rue readers to avoid ell cheep bulky packets and loose Bak- ing Powders, and Insist upon having Borrrick's.</p>
        <p>BOY AND GIRL LABOUR.</p>
        <p>I No alteration is proposed in regard to the employment of boys in mines. The present law is that no boy under fourteen, and no girl or woman of any age, may be employed in a mine below ground. The age for the employment of boys and girls above ground is thirteen, and no boy, girl or wuinan shall be employed about a mine for more than fifty-four hours a week or more than ten hours in any one day. There is a new pro- vision, however, affecting the working con- ditions of boys, girls and women, which proposes that the manager of every mine shall fix in the mine a notice setting out the hours of employment of these persons, and the times which are allowed for meals, and no boy, girl or woman may be employed except in conformity with this notice. THE CHANCES OF THE BILL.</p>
        <p>These are all the changes of importance which this new Bill proposes to make. As to the value of them in reducing the risks of coal mining, only the practical men with experience of their work can decide. The Bill passed the Second Reading in the House of Commons without a division, but not without indications that there will be strong opposition from the mine owners to certain , proposals of the Bill. The Miners' Federa- tion have been very fully considering the measure, and a number of amendments wil I be made in Committee with a view of bring- ing the measure more into line with the reforms which the men desire to see carried out. The Bill, as has been said, consolidates existing enactments with the exception of the Mines Eight Hours Act and the Check- weighing Act. It was probably thought that it was not desirable to raise the Eight Hours question again just now while the irritation caused by it is still being felt. The Bill will pass. The recent terrible accidents have silenced somewhat for the time being the cry about protective legislation increasing the cost of production to a prohibitive point. If the new Bill is going to do something to make mining safer, the public will welcome and support the Bill.</p>
        <p>i</p>
      </div>
