      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>0 OTISA ILL THUTI</head>
        <p>0 OTISA ILL THUTI</p>
        <p>Notes by Ovalist."</p>
        <p>The fir.t-class football season at Warrington con- cluded on Mortar, when the Wirepullers completed their fixture tat by eat, rtaibing llodder.held. This iA usually a fine match and that on Monday was no exception, th,mgh by way of a change the bulk of the scoring was done by (i.e visitors. A year ago War- rington won the corresponding fixture try It points to 5. On Monday, Huddersfield triumphed by :10 points to 8. The difference in the two results indi- outs potty plainly how much Warlingion base deteriorated this mason, and how much tl.e Forum° men have improved.</p>
        <p>After the display against Halifax on Ccod Friday, it MIS th tight that Warringtoa stood a good chanre ..hecking the a'l-conquerirg career of the Tykes, who hal a tun of 14 successive League victories to their credit. But the character of the opposition proved to be altogether difbrrnt. Huddersfield to:k command from the start, and the brillianco of their combination, coupbd with the pace of the men individually, caused Warrington's efforts to look pony in comparison. Finer running and pa-sing baa riot been a en at Warringto.i for many yeaTs, and the spectators were loud iii ticir praise of the great thirteen which the Fartown directors hove got vgether.</p>
        <p>It seems the irony of fate that Huddersfield sill have tog, barren of all honours this season. The difficulties of the early part if the season hive handicapped the team too much. Though every League match has fires won since January. the team is still outside the first four, and that unlm.lty miatake at Wigan knocked them tut f the Cup Competition. At present Huddersfield and Wigan are in a class by thern,lvea, and it is a thousand pities that they have riot to meet again in one of the deciding pines during the neat fortnight.</p>
        <p>During the week-end inotches fludderafiell litre beaten Wakefield by 41 points, Warp iogton by 31 points, sad Broughton hy 47 poinPa, an aggregate of gr ale, 27 tries, 112 points for the t'preeinute,es Conli any other team in Vie Union Lave equalled such a performance'</p>
        <p>Warrington did not play anything like as badly as the score en Monday avonld suggest. They had a g-'o! share of the game, bat were very weak on 1110 attaek compared with the victors. Lack of tare in the threequarter line Was the chief Canne. Warriegton were never dangtrous except when near the line, whereas the visitors were just as likely to get clean away and scare trlien operating in their own quarter. Most of thtir tries were fr in movements wldeli traversed over ba'f the length of the hell, while Warringt in's to itary cottple came from dashing hhort-range elf arts by Brooks.</p>
        <p>The litter was the heft man on the home side, and it is a pity he should only have found his form at the tag-end of the season. Creevey was weak in com- parison s ith re‘Mus efforts, and the remaining honours go to Tilley, who played a fine game at full back under great pressure. The forwards on either aide were well matched in scrummage work, hut in the bore e Huddersfield six were vastly more clever and speedy. A half. toe, Davies aid Grey gave n disp'ay worhy of Thunaa and G:eave, while the three-quer t.rs we: e a superb quartette, with Rosenfeld, the dashing Anstralian, a good first. One need hard), add I .at the match made it absolutely clear that Werriagloii will area faster hicks next season to stand any chance of holding their own in tither League or Cup tournaments.</p>
        <p>The League table up to and including Ilon.l.iy's inDtrlies is as follows : . .</p>
        <p>For. Age. Per- P. W. L. D. 0. T. P. 0. T. P. cent. Oldham 74 29 5 1.72 99 431.39 41 210... 83.82 6Vigan._ 34 28 5 I_9l 152 618 .91 45 203_ 83.82 Walefleld sa 21 8 1...74 115 493 .48 56 261... 7421 Widne 31 19 El 3. 41 72 310...19 33 137.. 69.33 Hull 1.88 39 21 9 3..111 122 588._45 68 298... 68•14 Ibutslet _3l 21 13 0.69 101 411...59 91 349... 61.76 IludderafT43s 21 14 0..801 151 655..42 69 291... 60.00 ...... _36 20 13 9..63 108 450...52 81 317... 5972 Warringt'll3l IS II 5 _46 111 281...45 71 303 .. 56 4i Swinton 35 IS 1) 0...53 72 331..33 54 228... 54.54 Dewsbury .30 16 11 0...37 70288.. 57 73 333... 53-31 Halifax -.35 17 17 1.58 81 361-51 73 321... 50.00 Ityclidale 831 16 16 1...2.3 70 338 .49 79 313.. 50113 Leeds 32 15 13 2.00 77 351....39 77 338... 50 09 Keighley 29 14 11 1...39 51 241...69 108 447... 50.00 Salford - 822 14 16 2...43 81 335 53 77 337... 48.871 Batley 31 II 16 1._37 06 272 ..4.1 51 233._ 48771 !Walton 1330 13 16 1...34 11 387.47 63280... 45 00 Sr.llelens 33 14 18 1.. 51 91 315-.62 103 450... 45.03 Leigh 3l 12 19 0._41 41 214...02 76 327.. 3870 Bunco., _29 9 17 3_.27 58 228_50 76 321... 3630 Barrow 3l 10 19 1.. 33 65 201...57 90 384... 35.00 BradfortiS3l 10 2) 1.-29 3,1 160...61 86 3•0... 5381 York stertbrrT.l9 513 0...18 19 90 ..47 81 337 2777 Ebbw Val 29 8 21 0...19 42 164-42 09 291... 27.58 Coventry 31 624 1...44 61 280-82 117 5)5... 2006 Brarulay 32 5 23 1...30 30 150...71 124 520... 17'18</p>
        <p>An interesting situation arose as the result of the matches in the Northern Ccion league played on Monday. Oldham, 1 y suffering defeat at Broughton, not only list the chance of living vvith Wigan to; the Lancsaldre championship, but also lo,t their lead in the League itself, which they have held practically thrt ughout the season. They tied in the percentage with Wigan, basing identical figures in the table of ranches played, won tat, aid disco. According to rule sucther extra match was necessary t decide the nominal I. adership, and on Wednesday evening tl.e two t taus net against Wakefield, though the reason for taking them ti Yorkshire is not obvious. Wigan put practically a reserve team in the felt, and Oldham dill rot try. A farcioal exhibition ended with Wigan winners by 11-3. Wigan sill note meet Widnes, and I thlbatn racer Wakefield, in the semi-filial. to-day.</p>
        <p>Widnes, by their success veer Dewsbury take the 4ourth place in the table, haying a b!ightly better percentage than hull Kingston lt,vere, who have made great efforts in their last few matches. The IMeers, however, caned a question as to their right to have the abandoned match with Merthyr—it was abandoned owing to the South Wales clot; becoming defunct—c-mnted i,i thr ir favour. lite League Com- mittee decided agaim4 the claim of the Rovrra at a ,inecial meeting in Tuesday, and Widnes will now einta,t t':e League semi-final--a worthy tribute to their pluck, yerseverance, and good football all the season.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Thomas IVil.lish sens re elected church- warden at St. Peter's Church, Rochester. on Wednesday for the .53rd time, lie sae first elected in triO, not believed to be the senior churchwarden in England. LINSZID COMPOCND • IFICU Wm water I. an excel Jett gs,gle or Sore Thrust, Colds, Coughs, .gc.—Advt</p>
