        <p>David 'I borne., inturance egent, Itegent.street, Earlestown, and his wife apprared before the St. Helens magistrate. on Monday. 'lite husband was charged ith wife *nation, end the case Lad been adjourned a month, with en order that he silt tiM pay los. n week. This bad been done, and the magistrates ordered another remand.—Mrs. Thomas strrnuously protested that she could not keep herself nod the children on lOrt —The Clerk t It is your mlnfortune. It in just like falling sick or losing your money. It is one of lila., things that cat be helped in life. ST. JOHN'S CHOIR IN THE CRCCIFIXION."</p>
        <p>A high class performance of Stainer'." Crucifixion " was given by the n.enrhore of St. John's Church Choir, Karlestxen, no Sunday eve, ing last week. There was a large congregation, and nothing but the earnest praise ens lire rd of the vast improvement made hy the choir tinder tie skilled training of Mr. C. "Meson, supplentented by tLe eerneat efforts of Mr. Lewis the new rogtnist. Mr. J. W. Willett, of War- ' rington, took ths tenor solos, and nag in a nay which proclaimed hint a gifted vocxlist. Ilia higher notes were really good, sod there was generally a melodione- ness and sympathetic expression which did jostice to his lint. Mr. James "ninon, of Warrington, proved a powerful end careful bass, whiltt Mr. Bonn and Mr. C. Appleton were allotted minor parts. In every choir one can always find some room for improvement, hint for a Church of Its vine, St. John's IN at present admirably equipped musically.</p>
        <p>NECILF.CT OF CHILDREN AT NEWTON. At the Liverpool County Sessidns on Wednesday Nora Miteinness (26), of Newton, was indicted fox neglecting her three children, •ged live, three,tand one ye, resp,ctivelv, in a manner likely to 1-1.11941 them unnicessary softening oninjinry to breath. Mn. Singleton who prosecuted, called evidence to show that owing to the drunken habits of the prison, the home was shockingly dirty and the children madly neslocte I. Prisoner's hoshand biters,' to give evideecs ocher behalf, but he wan declared to be intoxicated, and ordercd to stand down, his expenses as a witness being . . . .</p>
        <p>I he jury found the prironer guilty, but on account of the environment, recvmmended her to mercy. She was bound over to Loot good behaviour until next oesainne, when fte cave will be reviewed. A h age of being habitual drunkard WWI cv itbdr.iwn.</p>
