        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS NEW MEDICAL OFFICER.</p>
        <p>D. S. J. C. Holden, house surgeon of the South Dispenriary, Liverpool, has been appointed assistant medical officer sni schools medical officer for St. Helens. Dr'. Hoick n is an 111.11, Ch.B., D.P.11., of Loverpool University, and he bag filled the position of carnality officer and house surgeon to the 11iyal Infirmary. There cent largo number of smilicinth HANDSOME HIFI 10 11lE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>MARK'S CHURCH, ST.</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. W. hetchlse vicar, presided at the vestry meeting for this parish on Wedneaday itirht. Sir David lismlde, Tit are warden, preaented the recounts, which showed that the debt of £53 in. lid, on the church accounts hyd been wiped out, and there was • balance in hand of .E 5 Is. lee nion, re appointed Sir [Livid Gamble, Bart.. as churchwarden, and expressed his gratitude to hire for all he had (1.100. dlr. itubett Sterritt wee reelected people's warden. The sidesrnen were rwslectad, ith the substitution of Mead,. Logan and Ilerntwon in place of Mesa.. T. Phillips and W. Ashall jun. . .</p>
        <p>the Vicar n'n'nottoced that the Dowager Lady Gamble bed promised to pot up a new pulpit in the church in memory of the Into vicar (the Rev. George Lehrman, KG.) She was having designs prepared, and within:a few months they might hope to see the pulpit put in. Ile proposed that rtvotnrnpreoaiog the apurecietion cf the vestry be passed to Lady Gamble, and that the wardens be authorised to apply for a faculty.— Mr. Sterritt seconded, and the vote of thatilts can ueanimouely accordei.—Mr. Cook suggeited that the new pulpit and chancel would draw attention to the other part of the church, told he itiggeste I thet Borneo thing should be done to pit the nave in hatmony with the new et ttk.—Sir David tumble Raid that the choteS did need cleaning badly.—Mr. S. 1103 too proposed, and Mr. IV. Belabor aecondeal, that the warden, liner tl e work done, and this was agreed to. Votes of th.nits to the °nicer; and chairman closed the proceedings. 1310 AMY CHARGE AT Sr. lIELENS.</p>
        <p>At St. Ileiens, on Saturilyy, Alice Parrett, whose ! addriss was given an 11, Cernetery-road, York, was charged in custody a ilk bigarny.—ilhomas Edward William, 69, Emily-street, Thetto Heath, collier, said he ninths the acquaintance of prisoner at Leigh in August 1905. She gave the name of Alice Webster. On December 93, 1905, he !nerriel her at Cinlliorne Church. She told hint that she walla singe woman, and that eke had note relative or friend in the woild. Shortly after [carriage she told hind she had a child, which was at Yoik with a friend. Witnea liticame suspicious, and pressed her for tanker informa- tion, and in fhe c 111rse of time she told hitn that she had another chili at Halifax. He went there with her nod discovered that she had heen married before. In October, 1909, he got to know deflnittly that her husband wan slice. At that time they were living at Shotton Bridge, in Durham, and she left Lirn. If, followed her to York, and found that her hoiband (a soldier is tile West Riding Regimen- lives still alive. He also went to Halifax and got definite information of the first marriage.—Detective Anders spoke to executing the warrant at York, when prisoner replied, " Lam guilty. A soldier wet rosin Leeds, sad told roe that, my husbeed had been shot in India, boa did not make any icgitiries to see if it ass true."—The Clerk asked Williams why he had delayed so long taking nut the warrant. —William, anid he had the idea that it would cost a lot of tuonqy.—Prieoner said she told Williams all shout it.—She was committed for trial at the maize,.</p>
        <p>Prisqner was brought up at Liverp.4 .1,i,e4 on 11.uraday. . . . . .</p>
        <p>A sister of the prisoner sold that llst tilt left her sister destitute when be joined his regiment and went to India. Iler second husband, Williams, inns idle, and prisoner bad had tofu out to work to provide food for the house. Ile also ill-treated her. The Judge mold he accepted her story that her husband bed left her destitute. fie also believed that it was not her fault thnt she had not lived with the other man. She would be sentenced to two ria3n' Imprisonment, which would mean that she would be released on the rising of the court that day.</p>
      </div>
