      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SA lURDAY. APRIL 22 1911.</p>
        <p>We have just had THE DISRAELI another celebration of LEGEND. Primrose day. It is now thirty years since the death of the remarkable Statesman whom the somewhat forced imagination of the Tory party has converted into a sort of mythicel hero. He is now almost at historic distance. It is possible to judge of his character and his achievements without passion or prejudice. To us it seems that the achievement which far outshone all the rest was the reconstruction of the Tory party. Even so late as the years just before the Conservative victory of 1874, there were influential members of the Conservative party all over England who hoped above all things that a Tory triumph at the polls would carry BENJAMIN DISRAEL/ Into power. That was not the predominant feeling, of course ; but it was powerful enough to seem strange in the light of subsequent events. The hero of Primrose Day overwhelmed the old Toryism when he so memorably defeated Mr. GLADSTONE thirtyseven years ago. Were the old Tories right or wrong in their objection to him? From their own point of view they were unquestionably right. They were a stolid class of men, no doubt. They may even have been " tupid " as JOHN STUART MILL declared. But they had without doubt a genuine perception of the fact that the ascendancy of BENJAMIN DISRAELI meant the total submergence of the set of ideas on which their moral and intellectual edifice was built.</p>
        <p>What they feared came to pass. Where there had been solid ground, there was thenceforward a greater area of shifting sands. The most tremendous of the lessons learned from Mr. Dlsesitli when he " educated his party" was the unprofitable- ness of adhering to principles if such a pro- ceeding stands in the way of accession to power. It is our own opinion that this lesson has been carried much further than was intended. When Lord -.SALISJERY said at Liverpool many years ago, "We</p>
        <p>are going down a toboggan slide," he was acknowledging the extension that had I been given to the Disraelian method of "getting on." Mr. CHAMBERLAIN was just becoming a power on the Conservative side and since that time the toboggan slide has become quite a customary method of progres- sion among one school of politicians. The Disraelian lesson has been learned too well. The wearing of primroses on the 19th of April has become a quiet observance,indulged in to some extent by both parties, but most of course, by those who now acknowledge Mr. BALFOUR as leader. In thirty years there grows up a new population. It is safe to observe of most of those who wore prim- roses on Wednesday that they had in their minds no clear idea of a Statesman whom they honoured or of the work that he had done.</p>
        <p>No reputation is more evanescent than that of the average politician. Ever where a Statesman stands out as colossal in his own period as the late EARL OF BEACONSFIELD did his fame speedily dwindles in a world that has its eyes fixed on living men. Then, too, the achievement that seemed great often sinks into insignificance when the hard light of reality plays on it. Year after year we read in Conservative journals which keep up a belated enthusiasm, that Mr. DISRAELI won the sympathies of the Colonies for the Mother Country. But we remember how he said that the Colonies were a millstone around our neck. His most generous euoligst was Mr GLADSTONE, who said, "I can conceive Lord BEACONFIELD'S admirers as perfectly justi- fied, when greeting his return from the Prussian capital with the Treaty, in their application to him of Virgil's words, descrip- tive of Marceline with the spolia opima towering over all the companions of his greatness." What are the spolia opima now? How much of the Treaty of Berlin remains? Which of the signatory Powers would put a single soldier in the field as the response to a solicitation to revive its force? Of the Disraeli legend little more than the Primrose Day celebration remains.</p>
        <p>Father Wilson voices, 1 THE CORONATION in our correspondence I AND columns, the misgiv- WALKING DAY. ings which must te felt, by a large number of I people at the proposal to celebrate the Coronation and Walking Day on suctessive days. Not only will children and helpers be I fatigued after the loyal celebrations, but the difficulty of organising the Walking Day procession for the following morning is one that Father Wilson specially and rightly emphasises. We cannot agree with him, however, that the way out of the difficulty is to abandon the historic Walking Day procession for this occasion. Rather would we suggest the re-consideration de novo of the whole matter with a view to moving Walking Day back to its proper date, Friday, June 29th.</p>
        <p>When the House of S'ERENUOUS Commons reassembled COMMONS after its brief holiday, PROGRAMME. Mr. Asquith laid before it a strenuous pro- gramme to be got through by Whitsuntide. The Parliament Bill is to be passed through all stages in the Commons. Mr. Lloyd George's Bill for the establishment of sick, invalidity, and unemployment assurance is to be read a second time and sent to a Grand Committee. A Bill dealing with the law as created by the Osborne judgment is to be read a second time and sent to a Grand Committee. And a new Standing Order will be introduced exempting the debates on the adjournment for the holidays from the "gag' of blocking motions. In addition to this, Mr. Churchill has introduced a Bill to strengthen the deportation clauses in the Aliens Act, as many judges and magistrates seem unwilling to put them into force. With an important measure of social reform before it, as well as the Parlia- ment Bill, the House of Commons necessarily has to agree to a drastic curtailment of the opportunities of private members to ventilate grievances or bring forward legislative pro- posals of their own. The situation at the present moment is exceptional, owing to the constitutional issue. But whenever a Government is in office which is in earnest about reforms, and not content merely to mark time,private members will be obliged to make large sacrifices until some system of devolution frees the Imperial Parliament from its present enormous congestion of purely local business, and leaves it unembarrassed to devote itself to really large questions.</p>
        <p>Lord St. Aldwyn and EXTRAORDINARY Mr. Balfour have been</p>
        <p>LEITERS. writing some extra- ordinary letters as a contribution to the literature of by-elections. Lord St. Aldwyn says that "perhaps even the Monarchy itself" may b 3 abolished under the Parliament Bill. Mr. Balfour declares that, when the Bill is passed. Ministers intend to carry Home Rule "behind the backs of the people' and "without the knowledge and approval of the con- stituencies." Opponents of a measure, when they are gravelled for lack of genuine matter, invariably fall back upon some wildly improbable consequence that may flow from it. It is far wilder to suggest that the Parliament Bill might at some remote period lead up to the abolition of the Monarchy than it would have been for opponents of Mr. Balfour's Education Bill to accuse it of paving the way for ruin- ing all the old religious tests. But what would Mr. Balfour have thought of such an argument? When Mr. Balfour talks of Home Rule being carried " behind the backs" of the people, he should be confronted with Lord Ancaster's statement in the Referendum Debate in the House of Lords that, under the Parliament Bill, it would be more difficult to carry Home Rule than by a Referendum.</p>
        <p>The Irish Unionist IRISH TORIES Alliance- -one of the IN ENGLAND. strongest of the Irish Tory organizations— has issued its report, in which it is stated that at the last General Election that body placed in every county in England, and in chosen parts of Scotland and Wales, Irish Unionist speakers of repute, and trustworthy canvassers, who were at the disposal of local Unionist agents. It is also stated that ' immense quantities of selected pamphlets and leaflets were sent in response to requests from British Unionist agents." This body pretends that its exertions were made solely in the interests of Unionism. The report does not state the part that these speakers took in England, Scotland, and Wales with reference to the campaign</p>
        <p>in favour of Tariff Reform and against the Budget. It is as well that British electors should remember that these Irish orators who come to address them are more Tory than the Tories themselves. In no place in the British Isles is the campaign in favour of the House of Lords being more artfully and strenuously carried on than it is in Ireland, and it behoves the British electors always to remember that the Irish Tory, even more than the English Tory, would put back the hands of the clock, if he could, three hundred years. His ideal of the constitution was the ideal of the Stuarts, and he does not pretend anything else.</p>
        <p>By the death of the Earl of Carlisle and the succession of Lord Morpeth to the peerage, a Parliamentary vacancy is created in South Birmingham, and this brings the number of bye-elections pending up to four, namely</p>
        <p>Birmingham (S), Conservative majority,2,77B. Cheltenhani—Libnal majority 93. Dorsetshi re (El. Liberal majority 553. Barnstaple —Liberal majority 892. Viscount Morpeth has represented South Birmingham since 1904, with majorities ranging between 2,000 and 3,000.</p>
        <p>In the annual report of the County Federa- tion of the National Union of Liberal Clubs, the executive state that" rightly used our .cubs stand without fear and without reproach ; rightly regarded,they are healthy, wholesome providers of social intercourse, mental and moral improvement, and national recreation, for all their members rightly recognised they must be the acknowledged .centres of social, civic,and political education ,and activity. That our clubs are rightly used, rightly regarded, and rightly recog- nised in the host (and, th('refore,the strongest) Lib,ral districts are facts beyond dispute."</p>
        <p>While we are pleasCl to see the Rector of Warrington taking a personal interest in the tone of public entertainments we regret that he should have fallen foul of La Milo, without seeing the lady. We think it was due to her, and to the high position of the Rector as a custodian of public morals, to visit the Hippodrome and see for himself how far the living statuary exceeds the canons of good taste and seemliness. There are two sides to this, as to other questions, and apparently Canon Willis has heard only one side, and neither seen nor heard the other. La Milo's entertainment has the imprimatur of Mr. W T. Stead's apprcval among others, and to balance matters we hope the Rector will take the remaining opportunity of seeing the lady in her "expositions of classic art."</p>
        <p>. - After a course of lectures on " the nature of alcohol at a number of the elementary schools in Warrington and District, the scholars were invited to send in essays on the subject. Out of 1317 scholars who heard the lectures, no less than 712 submitted -essays, and 548 gained certificates. This result speaks well for the value of the lectures, and the interest shown by the scholars. One can only marvel more and more that in a fit of crass prejudice the Board of Guardians should have forbidden the giving of similar lectures to the children of the Padgate Industrial Schools. The Guardians are not doing their duty to the rising genera- tion in keeping these youngsters ignorant of the nature and effects of alcohol. There may be a certain amount of selfishness, as well as ignorance behind such an attitude. If the next generation grew up teetotal there would probably be little call for Workhouses, Industrial Schools and Guardians!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>, We understand that the recent census reveals the fact that the population of War- rington is 72,200—which is pretty much according to the general estimate, and compares with 64,242, at the 1901 census. Clause 1 of the Parliament Rill was added to the Rill at four o'clock on Wednesday morning after the customary protests from the Opposition, with Lord Hugh Cecil, as usual, as principal protester.</p>
        <p>Mr.C. J. Stewart, the Public Trustee, who is' 'ng so generally congratulated upon the re +able growth of the work of 64 depart- me , has been approached with even more cur,uus requests than some of those men- tioned in his last report. One client, seventy years of age, considered that it would come within the purview of the department to assist him in finding a sporting antagonist of similar age whom he wished to box, run, or jump for £lOO.</p>
        <p>The first woman president of the National Union of Teachers, Miss Cleghorn, of Sheffield, delivered her address at the open- ing of the annual conference of the Union, on Monday at Aberystwyth. In outlining a perfect system of schools, she dwelt on the importance of the infant schools, called for the abolition of half-time, and the raising of the school age and advocated variety in types of schools and preparation in school for industrial life.</p>
        <p>"For every dollar the present tariff puts into the producing industry, it puts at least three dollars into the pockets of the pro- tected interests." So says Mr. E. C. Drury, Master of the Dominion Grange, according to a report in the Toronto " Globe"; and this is the real secret of the agitation for Protection in this country. A few favoured interests seek the opportunity of lining their own pockets at the expense of the great mass of the consumers.</p>
        <p>.Those of our readers who are interested in Esperanto will be interested to hear that Mr. G. E. Pitt, F.R.S.S.. Professor of French at the King Edward VII. Grammar School, Birmingham, after a few hours study of the International Language, succeeded in obtain- ing the Diploma of the British Esperanto Association. He afterwards expressed the opinion that for all practical aims and purposes, Esperanto is streets ahead of any natural language.</p>
        <p>Mr. Harold Smith in cross-examin- ing a witness in the Warrington County Court on Thursday elicited the disconcert- ing information that a slaughtered horse was consigned to Germany for the purposes of making German sausages. This little " borne '. truth gave the lie direct to many , statements of the faithful band of Tariff Reformers in Warrington who in December last were PO diligent in trying to impress upon sane electors that horseflesh was an unknown quantity in Germany' • • . , The absurdity of the "Annexation" cry, to which we referred in a recent issue, is dealt with in a United States newspaper, "The Public," published in Chicago. "It is doubtful" says the writer, "if a thousand votes for annexation could be got in all Canada." Thus we see that both Americana and Canadians ridicule the idea of the annexation of Canada by the United States. It is only in the heated imagination of the members of the Tariff Reform party in this country that the " annexation " bogey exists. • •</p>
        <p>A peer, anxious to prove to his audience that he was descended from some civic dignitary who held office in the City of I London centuries ago, began, "You have all hesrd, I suppose, of Dick Whittington— thrice Lord Mayor of London (loud cheers); well, I am not descended from him, but—" " From his cat," piped out the voice at the back of the hall, and the ensuing tumult of laughter denied us the pleasure of learnntg what post his ancestor had occupied.—'"Humours of English Elections," Mr. lan I Malcolm, M.P„ in the "Nineteenth Century."</p>
        <p>If ever men presented a spectacle of cheer inanity it is now—when, having at long last triumphed in their struggle to subordinate to their welfare the uncompered element, they have straightway commenced to defile that element, so heroically mastered, by filling it with engines of destruction. If ever the gods were justified of their ironic smile —by the gods, it is now . . , . Water and earth are wide enough for men to kill each other on. For the love of the sun, and stars, and the blue sky, that have given us all our aspirations since the beginning of time, let us leave the air to innocence.— John Galsworthy in the_"Times."</p>
        <p>The political views of the late Earl of Carlisle were totally opposed to those of his wife during the last twenty-five years, although their married life has been more than ordinarily a happy one. Before became into the :title of Earl he sat as member for East Cumberland in the House of Commons, and his Toryism is of the old-fashioned type, unswerving and rigid. To him the House of I Lords was an object of veneration, the chief pillar of the Constitution. He believed in the necessity of a class distinction, and in the arictocratic creed as it was held by his forefathers before him. He was opposed to Woman Suffrage.</p>
        <p>The following estimates of a few of the revenue producing departments owned by the Burnley Corporation are interesting, in view of the financial position of the Warrington Corporation Gas cattribution to mite, of rat.,. £I ,Os) Electricity 14,000 In addition to the above handsome profits handed over to the relief of the rates, gas has been reduced this quarter 2d. per 1,000 feet, and electricity 1,4 d. per unit, which reduc- tions are equivalent to a ad. rate. The rates for the Borough of Burnley are 6s. 6d. illt the £ against 7s. 10d. in Warrington and Blackburn. It is rather a curious circum- stance that the Liberals have been in a majority on the Burnley Council for some years, whereas the Tory party dominates the municipalities of Warrington and Blackburn. ' Tamara.: a Now LJesid Gno•ot for Magna' sad Mak*. tabu Id.. M. lid, "—Ad".•</p>
        <p>LOCAL PARS.</p>
        <p>Personal</p>
        <p>In the Oxford Flonoura List (Claes IV.) pablialnA ,m Tuesday appears the name of James 11. Dalds. 'Fr laity Colhge. Oxford. ills third son of be late Mr. James Dodds of reakrth.</p>
