        <p>The last Band of Hope entei taint-I-tent in connection With the Brick-street took place on Thursday evening last, when a most enjoyable programme was gone through I.y the following members of the Band of Hope. Chairman, Mr. William Wright, recitations by Elsie Hardman, Both Monks, James Stringer, Miss Hannah Bibby. Ethel Owen, Jane Bibby; songs by Slits 11. Howell, Elsie Hardman, Edward Stitton (comic), Thomas Bibby ; pianotoite selections, Alice Harding ; duet, Phillii Ingham and Elsie Barton ; gramophone selections, Arthur Bibby. DREnO BASKET!4, WO, 21 6, 246. Special value in Hand Rigs, Trunto, Suit Cues, etc.—J. S. GARLAND, 31 LO 55, Blidg. et., Worrington.—Advt. Peat Fire Near Warrington. . .</p>
        <p>A lacge portion cf the°. expanse of peat lying between Glazebrook and Padget° iron destroyed by lire on Wednesday. The moss in worked by the British Miss Litter Company, of Thorne, Ponmiter. and is used chiefly for bedding fc, horses. The Warrington Fire Brigade put in an appearance, but 'they were unable to render any ansintance, because there was no water supply within a mile. There son. very little blase, the stacks smouldering canning .a dense volume of smoke to drift across the country- side, making it extremely unpleasant for field-workers.</p>
        <p>MOTOR BICYCLES. Sole agent for the celebrated Premier' Machine. Trial. rend all information free . —J.S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge Ms, Warringtoo.•Acirt Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., Congratulated at the</p>
        <p>Seasloos.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Sir William For vood), addressing Mr. Harold S.nith, M P., who had just made an appli- cation while holding his first brief in the Court of Quarter Sessions for the West Derby Hundred on Wednesday, said the bench were pleased to see him there. Many a great career at the Bar had teen etitterl at the Liverpool County Sessions, and the Bench wished Mr. Harold Smith every success.—Mr. Harold Smith, M.l'. ; I thank you, sir, very much. lie Nature of Alcohol. Ass sequel to the lectures which have recently been given in some if the day schools of this district on the Nature of Alcohol, under the auspices of the Lancashire and Cheshire Bond of Hope Union, a number of essays have teen submitted for examina- tion. The result is highly satisfactory, both to the children concerned and to the lecturer. From the report we find that tire number of lectures given was 12. The Lumber of scholars present, 1317. The number of essays submitted, 712, of which number,' 548 gained cerrificates. The schools visited were Padget., Wralston, Orford, Hollins Green, Great Sankey (Council), Penketh (Council). Penkerh Friends, Warrington A rpley.t reeil. Wa r r Mgt us (Surer-street. Wellington (Wycliffe), Warrington (Bolton ('ouncil) White Gloves at the County Sessions. At the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday, the Chairman (Mr. Charles White), was made the recipient of a unique presehtation, in the form of a pair of white gloves, in celebration of the fact that there were no cases down for trial. Mr. White was accompanied on the Bench by Councillor Henry Roberta and there were also present in the Couri.Mr. Henry Oreenall (clerk to the justices), Mr. T. P. Bradshaw (chief clerk) and Inspector Lewis, of Earlestown. Mr. Henry Greenall in making the presentation, said that it was only the second time during his term of office as clerk,which extended over 24 veers, that he had known such a presentation The Chairman very briefly responded.</p>
