      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>..._ _ IRISH AFFAIRS. NOTES FOR IRISH READERS. )</head>
        <p>..._ _ IRISH AFFAIRS. NOTES FOR IRISH READERS. )</p>
        <p>AT WESTMINSTER,</p>
        <p>The political -a-t-rnokiire're-inei-no-W—is a little trying. The people who sent the present Government back to power t put the Parliament Pill on the Statute IS,ok, are by this somewhat irnpatieht at the rope which its being given to the opposition in their frivolous ober rue- tive tactics. Irislou-n, h ,terser, have • grain of satiefaction in the thought that the Irish Parliamentary Party are giving practical proof of their earnest- nesm in tho Veto stloggle. The other day for example about half the Party were in attendance at Westminster. At two d tye notice the full strength of its membership was in evidence in the division 1 lobbies on Thursday if this week. When it is ' remembered that the absentees left pressing business matters in Ireland and crossed over for the one object of voting at Westminster it will be clear what greet persomil sacrifices are being made by men who are not too well blessed either with leisure or wealth. Fir ' Edward Grey's speech te other day on the subject of the control of the House of Commons over treaties and foreign affehs genoragy showed Once more that in high politics all roads lead to Ireland. Mr. Sr ift Mac Neill bad kdicated the close relation betAventhe American Arbitration Treaty end Irish friendliness, but Sir Edward Grey went much further and frankly stated that the House of CLmmons could not hope to give proper attentha to f,vign and Imperial affairs until it relegated fo I:eland and other natioaalitivs the management of their domestic affairs. THE POSITION OF Tfll TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>Two or three °mimesesa ef diverse interest and impart.ce are to be held this week in Ireland, for of recent years the C114.0M has grown up in that country as in Great Britain, for public holies to assemb'e in conference Au Easter week. Fitly enough it is in Bangor of ancUnt fame as a seat of leap sing, whose wholars covered Europe as missionaries and civil mere, that the Irish National Teachers' Organisation assembles for its annual congreaa this week. By pressure of the conditions under which they have to labour, inadequ..te pay, inadequate isension, a faulty system of promotion besides which the Holmes circular appears alnr at decent, in.ufficieney of equipment in the schools, by reas n of all these glaring detficiencies, the Irish teachers instead of addressing themselves at these c .nferenees to the se:ence of education and the problems of the schoolroom, are compelled every year to reiterete demands the justice of which no one denies. I.ike W. S. Gilbert's policarnan, the Irish teacher has " no happy lot:' Upon no hotly of tvorkers has so touch sympathy been lavished by the lepresentativtn of the Government, but that sympathy has yet to find practical expression. The Cbaucellor if the Exchequer, whose opfro telling recovery to complete health gratifies everyone, was aotonishet to find that the hard driven teacher in the primary school of Ireland was eo poorly remunera.ed. Teachers in Great Britain, with sitnilar qual.fications, receive salaries which are 40 per cent., and even 50 per cent. burlier than the incomes of their brethren in Ire'and. But the Irish worker, whose duties and responsibilities are constant'y increasing, is left to clamour lota decent wage, a pension th it will not he a 'rockery and for some consideration for the man or woman who lon t fall out of the ranks because of unavoidable breakdown after five or ten years' service. In the large agenda paper we observe there are no less than fourteen resolutions relating to thew and other quest'ons which 3ear after )ear have to be pressed upon the attention of Ministers, the Treasury, the ' Commissioners of Nwional Educati la. and the public. It is encouraging, however, to MA., that Mr. Aaquith hart promised the Leader of the Irish Party th it pro- , vision will be made for an early debate on Irish Primary Education in the noose of Commons. and we sincerely trust same definite steps may be taken towards the improvement of so important a sphere of the nation's life. The persistency f the Organisation in proclaiming these points cannot go unrewarded. Progress has been slow, Ind the rate has not dis- couraged the teachers, an I it la obvi am that no long as they feel the yoke so long trill the protest be con- firmed, even though no prospect of redress presented 'I hat is the right spirit, we feel .re, to manifest in a struggle Inc justice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>IRISH TOBACCO.</head>
        <p>IRISH TOBACCO.</p>
        <p>By directing the attention of Irish public boirds to the fart that excellent tobacco is now producel in 'lreland, the Irish Tobacco Growers' Association has taken an effective step to further the industry. In the ' circular addressed to Boards of Guardians and titer bodies in control of institutions where the "weed' is consumed, it is pointed out that tobacco cultivation is specially suited to small holdings and provides , employment for whole families including the boys and girls. Theref re, Irish representative bodies are well advised in doing all they can to assist this nascent industiy. A sound proof of the excellence of the !doh grown tobacco is to be found in the efforts which British manufacturers are making to crush the lush industry. Quite recently in Liverpo,l, an Irish supporter of the home- grown tobacco weld into a shop and asked focus ounce of Irish tobacco, and received an article which was later definitely ascertained to have been manu- factured elsewhere. Mr. Redmond might well press for the wider recognition of the Irish Trade Mark which would pnt • stop to the impostures, only too generally prevalent which impede the progress of birth industries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
