      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Groves—Walker.</head>
        <p>Groves—Walker.</p>
        <p>One of the most fashionable weddings, which have taken place in recent years at Lviiim, was aolernnized on Trieeday afternoon when Miss Frances Ethel Augusta Groves. daughter of Maj., J. F.. G. :roves, Dean. Green. Lynitu. was married to Mr. Jocelyn Lomax Walker, of Rerborotigh, Alberta, Canada, sec ond son of the late Mr. Thomas Walker, barrister- at-law of the Inner Temple and Mm. Walker, Maghribi, Isle of Man. The weather wee not all that could be decreed, but happily the rain kept off. The ceremony was performed in the prettily aituated Parish Church.' which wan resplendent with the beautiful Easter decorations The bride ia very well-known and highly esteemed in the district Led a large congregation awn:sided to witness the ceremony. The Rev, G. B. ThUrfirt .11, M.A. (rector), officiated. Mr. Cook, wlm y maiden et the organ, gave maieful interpretationa of the Bridal March horn Lohengrin all Mende/ssohn's " Wedding March," the latter being played ss the rarty left the Church. 'lke bride, who wan given away liy her father, wore a lovely Empire white satin dregs with ninon overall and trimmed with embroidery and pearl ! lloniton lace. TIIO tra.n, I ring from the should ra, was decorated with roar]k. her lloniton lace veil was surmount.' by a wreath uf orange bloasotn. Her orange. tt included a pearl and gold necklace, and she carried a bougnet of whi'e iii., lilac tad white heather. She was at ertied by the Idis‘ea Kathleen, Margie and Alma. Grow. (her sisters). ! Sybil Baldington (cousin of the b idegror oil and !Ilk's gmenie llodgLinson (cousin r,f the bride), while Master Sidney Groves (her ber flier) and Mina Rose- mary Elgood (her main) acted as train bearers. The bridesmaidm were gowned i I dreswes of pearl grey, shot w:th pink aid trimmed with blue satin and cp Id facings Tire r large white tegal hat. were ti inimed with roees and French grasses. They carried banquets of pink toe., and wore pearl and esarriel pendant', emblematical of Canada, the gifta f the biidegroom. The train Ware,n were dressed in white. The bride's mother's dress w n of mole satin with gold 1 tubi oidery facings, 111111 her Re 10 hat 11,01 trimmed w:th ostrich feathers. 'lke la Nem ootn's mother had a black satin dress trimmed with run le and a large block list, trinuned with r. see. Mr. Keith G. Groves acted as hest ntan and the grooms- Man Warl Mr. Lawrence Walker. The reception can hr lit at the home if the bride's peen* a navel and much-appreciated feature of the fr istivities being con- tributions on the harp by Mien Harrison, papa of the late Mr. J. Cockerill of Manchester. . .</p>
        <p>he following na hot of the invited girsts Mr Las renee Walker,Mr and Mrs James Grimble Groves, Dr nod Mrs lindgkit.son, Mrs F M Elgood, Mr and Ills II Leigh Grover, fir and Mrs Peer Groves, Mr Osborn Gloves, Mr Julian Groves, Miss for dhy Hodgkins., Mr ALIII Hodgkins., Mrs Walker, Mr T Wiltirsm Walker, Mrs and Misr Boddington, Mr Miles B ddingtort, Mr and Mrs Greenwood, Mr 0 Greenwood, Miss Greenwood, Mr and Mrs Miss Liles, Mr Wes, Mr Grey BAIL the Misses B yd, Mr and Mrs Charles II Leigh, Miss Leigh, Miss a,,? Mr Cronshaw, Mr H Roberts, fir Ernest Robert', the lie, 0 Ii Thurston, fir and Mrs Abercrombie, Mr and Mrs Humphrey, Miss lidomplirry, Dr and Mrs Burrowes, Mr and Mrs ('sllender, Miss Esme Callender. Mr and Mrs James Farrar, Mr and Mrs Forsyth, fir and Mrs Harold Johnson, Miss Eirg, Mi-s Lattice Muller, Captain and Mrs II H 31 Mo dy, Mr and Mrs Oswald Moseley, Mrs and NIMs Norris, Miss Lillie Ogle, Mr aud re Joseph Palmer, Colonel and Mrs Saner, the Rev H A I) Surridge, Misr Surridge, Mr Claris. Sorridge, Mrs Watkin, Mr Edward IVutkin, and the Ilea and Mrs E Werhered.</p>
