      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>PALACE AND -HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND -HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>The seclnd viii to Warrington of the famt.us posent.e. Is Milo, Itas aroused considerable interest, and large audiences have been the rule during the week at the winder Louse of entertsinment in Friars' Green. La Milo in a smelt-criticised lady, but she has been approved by over Ci Watt U Committees, which th all be 'tome sort of recommendation. Her</p>
        <p>living statuary expositions hare earned world-wide renown, and possess an artistic bonny •ml value whxli canner be denied except by the prejudiced or pornographic mind. The one 1111ng we quarrel with in regard to her entertainment in the description " an entirely nsx tepertms of pines" If we are not Rreatly mistaken all, rr nearly all, those which were unveiled to the audience on Tuesday, were the subject of similar treatment on the occasion of La litton first visit. With the lady is Cruickshank, the celebrated lightning cartoonist, who depicts famous political personages on the screen with a few deft strokes of the pencil. .1 hiogroph film also illustrate* " is day wCh Is Milo,- and a strenuotto day too, with the exception of tea in the garden with a eolitle of devotees of art who are conspicuously attired in clerical garb. The rest of the prcgramme this week 14 made up of o.cely-varied lets,. Helene and</p>
        <p>Emilon earn warm applaum for • daring and dexterous symnastie turn, and Les Wartenas t three young ladies) compel admiration for their dainty joggling. A aketeli on novel lines is presented ny Hiss Hose Hamilton and Co. with the title</p>
        <p>- •• Woman versus Man." The °Meals contains man ient realism and humour to be decidedly enter- taining to the audience. Will Talton and Jack Terry distinguish themselves with concerted step-dancing, and a bright turn is also provided by the Three Langfords in song and dance. Ross Warren perf•irms the comedian's part with fair enccess, and a good reception is accorded to Dave Carter, " the popular 'Fish tenor," for a couple of vocal ditties possessing that sentimental quality which feems tm be a sin ven non in the •• halls." Mr. If iddop's orchestra in excellent, as usual, and gives a spirited selection from " William Tell" Miring the interval.</p>
