        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE</p>
        <p>Mr. Alexander Marsh and Company</p>
        <p>in Repertoire.</p>
        <p>TM. has been .11' of the mnt enjoyable repertoire weeks ever provided by the management of the Royal Court Theatre, Mr. Alexander Mandl and his company having submitted a high-clam and well varied series of Shakeapmrein and atandar I play,. Mr. Marsh has previously appeared in Warrington with marked dis- tinction in Mr. Alan Wilkie', company, end in this enterprise of his own he ban sh Ira himself quite capable of fulfilling the most difficult dramatic rides. Ile is supported by that charming actress Miss Carrie liaillie and a strong company. 'Fire performances have afforded the unmet pleasure to the large andieaces, the thestm being filled to its utmost capicity on Bank Holiday evening. The following attractive plays have been presented : Monday, " David Garrick ' sod " The Sleigh Bethel; Tuesday, " Delicate Ground " and " The Corsican Brothers Wednesday, Virginius " ; Thursday, " A Winter's Tale " Fr iday, " The Three Musketeers." Mr. Marsh is undoubtedly one of the ablerk of our yr:ringer actors, and the leading parts in the above plays have afforded hitt ample [mope for the display of his exceptional talents. Ile never over- acts a pert, hut is alwaye aufficiently powerful, and lie is Weaned with a deep voice which he has under perfect control. Om of the swiftest cortein-raiaers we have ever seen in ' Delicate ground " which wan given on Tuesday craning. It is real but not too (limey CORI, dy, noun tip written. and it was delightfully meted by Mr. Marsh, kli-s Baillie and Mr. lan Fleming. " The Coriacan Brothers," which fo!lnwenl. in an old favourite and Mr. Marsh wan remarkably convincing in the &amp;ruble p rrt of Fabien and Louis, the twin Imrthers. Miss flullie gave a touching bur restrained portrayal of Emilie de Lesparre and Mr. Ernest Diggers was responsible for some distinctly clever work as If. De Chateau Renaud. The duel in the last act was not the toi obvions make-Ifelieve which often passes fur stage figlia, but a brisk and exciting encounter.</p>
        <p>On Thursday ereoing Shakespeare's "The Wiater's Tale " was enacted In-fore an audience rothing like OM numerous as the interest of this powerful play and the merits of the performance wontini have led one to expect. The tale i• ead'y pathetic at the culmination of the sec nil act, making up in more cheerful incident, en it thenceforward runs its c arse. Mr. Alexander Marsha presentment of " leontes " and the dual parts of " Ilermione and " Perdita " portrayed by Mies Carrie Raillie, were excellent in every way. T -night, "Othello" . NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
