      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>PENKETH HOUSEBREAKING CASE. OVERHEARD IN THE CELLS.</head>
        <p>PENKETH HOUSEBREAKING CASE. OVERHEARD IN THE CELLS.</p>
        <p>STORY OF A CONSPIRACY THAT FAILED.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool County Sessions, on Wednesday, Frederick (treaties (39), clerk, and IIIOIIIBC AtIIIMBOII (52), gardener, were indicted for breaking and entering the dwelling-bonse of harry Pitt Cunningham White, at Peoketb, between July 25th and August IBth, 1910, and stealing clothing arid a revolver and eeventy•five cartridges. (heaves pleaded guilty, and Adamson not guilty, either of the hr. alibir or of receiving. Mr. Ilmold Smith, M.P., for the prosecution, said the burglary took place while prosecutor and his family were anay holiday. In Adarnson'a possession while he was in cuatody for being drunk at Leigh bad Leen found a silver Gargle and charm, part of the proceeds of the robbery. Ilia own story efts that he found tie tangle at NlencLester. After Greases nntl Adams( n, alto were known to be close friends, had been locked up in eeparate cells at Sankey, they were heard carrying on a conversation in the Lancashire dialect, the gist of which was that Adllll3f OP was to plead not guilty, and by a mutually fitt tag account of the matter to be given in court, he would be likely to he discharged. (heaves gave evidence exonerating Adamants, and taking all the blame on himself. Ho declared that the cell conversation never took place. The jury found Adamson guilty of receiving the bangle, knowing it to Lave kern stolen, and he was rent to gaol for six weeks. Greaser, who pleaded that be men subject to delusions, wee sentenced for twelve lac at he.</p>
      </div>
