        <head>Borough _Police Court</head>
        <p>Borough _Police Court</p>
        <p>WerinisnAT.—Befote Mr. Cheeks White (in the chair) and Messen.J. W. Hayward and W. T. Williams. 'A BAD PREDI, " —John Corcoran, of no settled abode, was charged with getbering alma under falee pretences.—The Chief Constable said the //ripener 'was even by Sergeant Stuart in Orb rd-lore, going from door to door and preventing a piece of piper on which eves written the following : "I am inn bad predica- ment. I hat, loot hearing and speech from • revere fright. My mother is in the hospital and I have no friends. I depend upon people's generosity. I have , on/y been deer and dumb for three week,. Kind friends elen't let ore starve. I am sure God will reward you. Ton know I can never earn my living again." The whole thing was a fnbriention. Ile was not deaf nod dumb. He bad juot finished a sentence for forgery end the Chief Constable mggented that the men ehould he remanded foes week in order that further inquirien might In made.—Prisoner was accordingly reruended for seven day.. FROM At ROSS ORE 0 HERRIN" PONl4."—Frook Hale, of Philadelphia, America, wns charred with otealing on the Xed April, a parcel of clothing, value 2Ca., the property of George litirric—lt appenred that on Thurs- day morning, whilst prosecutor erns walking along Liverpootiroad in the direction of Warrington he was overtnken by the prisoner and they walked together to Wet rington. Prosecutor went into a pawnshop for the purpose of pledging a pair of hoots and left with the prisoner d parcel containing other clothing. On re turn• mg front the pawnshop, he found that the prisoner had decamped. Information was given to the police rend prisoner was arrested the fame night. He hail pledged articles nt pawnshops in Warrington. Prisoner raid he ceme over from An,erica by working his passage can mettle boat, but was unable to get shoal to return. It was his intenti, n logo to Southarepton to try ".o join a boat there.—ln view of the fact that he lead teen in custody for about a week, prisoner was discherged. TCESDA v.—Retire Councillor II Roberta fin the chair) •nd Mr. J. Rymer Young. Willis. Otte ens Wom nox Horror Y.—Joseph Merrell and Healy Marratt of Foundry.steeet were charged on remand cub being drur Ir.—The Chief C 4 notable otated that the prisoners had for Rome con- eiderable time been taking a large qoaettity of drink. About a quarter to trn on Wedneeday last they were mien by P.C. !Soo}, to leave a public-hoese inn very drunken condition, and he locked them op. 'I hey had been detained in the Police Station for eix nights end he would suggest a nominal fine would meet the crap. Mr. Steel, who Appeared for the prinonerr, sleeted that the men had unfortunately from time to time taken drink, although the care was one of eimple drunken- ness. They had Leen in prison SiDCA Wednesday and there wets also the fact that they had hoes in prieon while other people were on holiday. Ile would there- dole on three grounds suggest Het the eurnmons be withdrawn on payment of costs.—. 4 fine of 10e, each including costs lens impoeed. . _</p>
        <p>AtSRULTING his Wino—John William Jackson, of Cloth Hall-yard, was charged with aseaulting Ilia wife, Hannah Jackson, on the 13th inst. The complainant stated that on Thursday evening fast when she asked defendant for his money he refused to give it bee and avid that if the wanted morey the must gu and work tor it. Utter am name evening, while cutting up some tobacco, he rusted at her and the knife struck her wrist, all hough she would not ray it waft intentional. Ile bad also thrown thing, at her. All that she asked for was. reputation older, juat to keep him away from the house. Slitt did rot care how much money was granted.—The Chief Constable told prisoner had been 34 times previously before the Court on charges of drunkesness, larceny, he, the last time being in January when he was tined ss. and costs for being drunk and disorderly. Ile was alwaya to be lon nd in public-houses when he was not at work. Mr Firth. the Police Court Miesioner, eaid the prisoner was •• a brute of a man."—Prisoner said his wife had not leen poker for the last nix month.. The wound on her wrist had not heen inflicted by him. She was doomt pawning thinga.—Prisoner wan Itued Ws. and costa, with the alternative of days. DRINK IRE D.U"SE.—Jalilt9 Phillips, residing at 344, Hale-street, was charged with being drunk end di, orderly in Slick-street and alto with aasaulting P.C. Harrison. He pleaded guilty to the lirst charge tut not to 11.0 /iRrOI d. PC. Harrison said that about eleven o'clock the previous night whilst on duty in Brick-attest he saw the prisoner behaving ion very disorderly menner. %%hen in went to caotion him he snuck Lim a trident blow on He mouth. He was violent all the way to the Police Station end witness had to let the seristacce of an ollicer from the Itarracks.in Re araygle nit ncsa'a coat was torn in three places. The crowd was also vt ry disorderly. Lance. Sergeant Wetron,alationed at Orford Harracks,nnid that he went to the officer's afflatus. and helped to tabs the prisoner to the Poiles Station. The priaoner was very violent and the crowd was also very boat ile.—Prisoner raid be was very sorry for abet had occurred. Its ran drunk at the time oaks we old not here committed the aarault.—Tbe Chief Constable said be thought if the prisoner had been in • proper ware he would not have come hied the eamatelt.—lie wry thved 10a. Ind cotta or 14 der.</p>
