      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>-4 WARRINGTON Cnurch</head>
        <p>-4 WARRINGTON Cnurch</p>
        <p>r</p>
        <p>St. Matthew's Church, Streit., recut:lies a picturesque and commanding ioaition, and its tall tower is one of the roost prominent landmarks in that , pleasant stretch of rural Cht shire which lies FO Close to Warrington's borders. The Church was originally built by the Church Commissioners as achapel-of.ease Ilto Great Budworth and, with but ial ground, it wan consecrated by Bishop Blomileld in 1827. A special district was assigned to it in 1834. The late Ven.. Archdeacon Greenall built the present handsome chancel, and in 18110 an enlargement of the church- yard was consecrated. The nave, aisles and tower were rebuilt and consecrated in 1872, Mr. T. If Lyon, of Appleton Hall, being a large conttibutor Ito the scheme and also donor of the organ .The present Vicar of Stretton is the Rev. Hon. Charles Francis Cross, M.A., whose kindly sympathy, homely goodll nature, and d ep sincerity have endeared him to his I parishioners. He is the fourth but second surviving ; son of the first Viscount Cross, and nephew to Mr. T. 'lf. Lyon, aid was born ont September 22nd, 1860, in the house which is now the Vicarage, NOlthwich. Mr. Cross's early years were spent at Bill Chile, Warrington. lie scan first educated privately muter the late Bev. Canon Gray, Kirkby Vicarage, Liverpool, end then proceeded to the great Public School of Rugby. Ilia University course was undertaken at Trinity College, Cambridge, where he obtained the degree of M.A. Baying studied at Leeds clergy school, Mr. Cross was ordained and entered upon the curacy of St. John the Bar,tibt, Chester, which he held from 1884 to 1887. Ile was curate at New Brighton from 1887 to 1891, in which year lie , was appointed to the living of Shotwick, Mr. Cross , came to Stretton in 1897, and under his guidance the church has made steady progress in all departments of its work. Mr. Cross's sermons are on simple, evangelical lines and he is a very diligent visitor of his flock, lie takes a very keen interest in the Church Army and is adviser of the van work in tie diocese of Chester.</p>
        <p>Easter Day at the Parish Church. The eervices at the Warrington Parish Church on Sunday were of a joyous character, befitting the great festival of Bunter Day. The church hed been very tastefully decorated, the altar, with its wealth of choice white flower., presenting a beam iful appearance. The principal service of the day was the choral celebration of the Holy Communion in the morning, the choir giving a devotional rendering of Eyre', flue setting in E flat. The Rev. A. .1. Brett preached on Christ's appearances to Mary Magdalene mei Si. Thorn.. In the evening the anthem °Hod bath appointed a day " (Tours) was creditably sung and the service concluded with a magnificent reuderiog of the Ilallelukth ChOTUS. The Rev. Eustme Hill, of the Community of the ' Reeurrection, brought bit misaion to a clom with a powerful mrmon on the Reaurrectim, his teat being "Jesuit saith unto her, Woman, why weepeat thou? " After dealing with the evidences of the Resurrection, he urged the paramount importance of close personal relatiomhip with Christ. There were lame congrega- tions at all the services.</p>
        <p>ITL L.l 1.-111../C 0.1 , 14111"</p>
        <p>Photoi [Tmoge REV. HON. C. F. CROSS, VICAR OF STRETTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>St. Barnabas Church.</head>
        <p>St. Barnabas Church.</p>
        <p>The Easter ITn-Y services at this church Led a epecin interest, as the flue new organ which has been erected was used for the Ilest Gene. A full description of the instrument has already appeared in the column of the " Examiner." There are two manuals—great end swell —and pedals, tat the organ is so errenged that a choir organ and other enlargements can be undertaken with- out difficulty when occasion arises. The pedal action son the bent tubular pneumatic eystem. The organ has been built by Mew rs. Alen Young and Son., of Manchenier, end the handsome Care is to the design of Messrs. William and Seger Oxen. The instrument end the epecielly constructed gallery on which it stands have cost nbout RCM and it is gcatilr log to note that the whole of this amount hag already been raised. For Sunday's service a the church Mid been very Leautifully decorated with spring flowers. 11.0 organ was first used at a choral celebrat le n if the Holy Communion at 8 a.m., robes the church van well filled. The musical setting wan Simper in F e id it was very reverently sung by I the choir. At Matins the preacher was the Rev. 11. Ni. S. Banlihart who alto addressed n children's service in the afternoon. Al evansong the Vicar (lice. J. 11. G. Hollingwortii) preached and the choir rendered the anthem " Awake up my glory." li,. T. Evans presided at the organ at nIl the services.</p>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesley Guild. AP usual the membera of itewsev-rued Weeley Guild joined in a ramble on Enter Monday. About fifty of them met at the church et two o'clock und walked to Moore nit Arpley, Walton Arches and the canal. Tee was partelteu of in the Weslesan school- room after which some of the party sojyed feminine field, while others went fora stroll round the village.</p>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church. - On Easter Sundly evening the choir of St. John's gave a rendering of the cantata " From tanager to Frets" (Witt v), dercriptire rf be birth, ntlnistry, death and resurrection of Chtist. the rolo tarts were sung by Mesdames Miller, Altery, tad Deer, Mimes Precook and Snailbnm, tad Messrs Albery, Dean, Gilleepie,Mitchell and Mengel!. 'I he choir wax trader tbe leadership of Mr A G Lewsey, and Mrs J Snailhem nes organist. A short address arts given by the Itev.l W. Reid, on the subject of the Atonetneet.</p>
        <p>Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Church. On Easter Monday the Wesley Guild had its first ramble et He, season to Applett n, Mts. l'orslow's farm heirg the destination as in preview, rambles. Over GO members and frit nda started from Rank Quay aborts 12 o'clock. The parts' took the car to Latchford and then journeyed past party Church to Appleton which was reached in good time. Various games were indulged in by both sexes in afield kindly lent by Mr.. Putslow. The weather on the a hole was very Savour. able. A little rain was experienced in the afternoon but nothing to speak of. Tea was prepared at a nominal charge by Mrs. Poralow and over ler members did justice to the good things set before them. Others took their food with them and obtained tea and milk at the farm. After tea, game. were again indolged in till shout h o'ciock when the party congregated ta the farmyard and sang a few h3mns before commencing the homeward journey. The direct TCAIi wee chosen for home the party being (111 re or lege tired, Warrington being reached at 9.15, after a mt at enjojable dir)'s Outing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>LOGIC OF SWEETS.</head>
        <p>THE LOGIC OF SWEETS.</p>
        <p>If the perusal of these lines will induce our enter- prising housewives to make the " sweet " course dainty and attractive, and at the Same time economical, a good object will have been achieved. So frequently in the orthodox pie or judding monotonous and dis- tasteful, that no apology is needed for suggesting simple variants. For this we must get back to what logicians call " first principles. We do not believe the familiar taunt that women are illogical creatures. his a libel on the fair sex. Every woman knows that among the most pepular if all sweets are the Christmas pudding, mince pie, arid the fanious Eccles cake. Nur is the penny bun to he despised. All of these derive their filVOllMena from the little GI eek Currant which is the chief Ilavotping agent of the beat confections. More than that, it is very nutritious. Currant, have a natural sweetness derived from the rays of the non; they are full of grape sugar, tie finest form of oonceutrated (cud. Here we have a clench,. fruit Nature's oven nutritive sweet. The logical conclusion is to use it. There are countless ways of using currants, allot which are worth trying.</p>
      </div>
