        <head>WAIMINGTON PARISH CHURCII.</head>
        <p>WAIMINGTON PARISH CHURCII.</p>
        <p>• —••—••—••• The Easter cestry meeting was held in the Fairfield schools at noon on 'I 'wader, the Rector (Rev Canon It alio presiding neer a goOd attendance, which included the keys TA .1 Hides' h. T J Brett and T N'iokere, Mr Isherwood (people's warden) and Mr E A White (vestry clerk).</p>
        <p>TIIE scoors-rs. Mr E Isherwood (people'a warden) sal3 be could not any that he had great pies/cure in presenting the account; for the religion that they had en adverse Imlance. When they contidered that the Perish Church , wits eonewhat dependent for its maintenance neon Mlle of uork and parochial teas,te thought they would etre* that truth a state oft hinge ought not to ocher. Dining the year the collections for church expense. amounted to £293 111 10d, and for specie! porpoises to .1:261 IS. It,!, making total e ffertortes of £260 10a 7,1. It. taconite) from otter at urces Art.ounted to £142 Oc 4d and there wee a del.it balence of LID 12s 4d, es against • Molt of £76 5s 701 last year. The number of noir. contributed in thee ffertoriee nt the °relit ary chute h services doting tio year wes 64,451, and in addition there warn runt of £509. ltd collected at the early celetiratiovs. The offertories of which record had leen kept were made rip as follower-11041mnd. 12,456 ; pennies, 30,202; three-penny bile, 8,106; six- groove, 2,2112; ahillings, 925 ; florins, 120; half-crown. 150 ; row's, 1 ; helfmovereit rum, II ; sovereign., 21; and one ()high° for Ito. Tie average number of Coin. collected for church expellees had hosn 1007, and for special objects the a ;erne was 1074. Th• riverage amount of collectionc (or church expense. was £75. Brl and for epecial ohjects Ell in 9d. The leverage collar- . Mon all th• year round worked out at .88 lie 3d. The average si mount required per wok for church maintenance was £6 5,, Hie contention was that the church ought to maintain itself by its offertories. , Even excluding the soldiers and the scholars trots whom collectionn were not taken, they could Ott,! that at least 1,500 people worthipped rit the church ,ery Sunday. . . . . _</p>
        <p>Tbe itectO.r —then jhanded to Mr. Isherwood a cLeque for £2O which he bad received to clear off the deficit on the accounts. (Applause). Mr.T. Mason moved tie adoption of the accounts and emphasised the fact that the Parish Church had an almost exclusively workingclass congregation %villa wee not inn position to give very large amounts. Mr. S. Taylor seconded and the accounts were approved.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFIC Ile Rector nominated Councillor J. G. Ctaik an his warden for am they Pa r. He could not say limn intensely sorry he (the Rector) was thas serious illness prevented Commillor Craik.a attendance at that meet- ing. Ile felt deeply that his appointment of Coun- cillor Creik was one which met with the most perfect arid entire approval of the congregation. Ile win a second people's warden, because they all respected and loved him. (Applause). Mr. F. or, in proposing the re-election of Mr. Edward Isherwood as people's warden, said anyone who , had had the opportunity of noticing the official work at the church m uld not fail to have been impressed with the admirable stay in whith Mr. Isherwood dincharged his duties. . _</p>
        <p>The fa.ropositiot;;na seconded by Mr. Mason, suppor- ted by Councillor Locker eel unauintously and heal tile approved. The Hector read a lett, from Councillor Creik ' thanking him for his re-appointment as Rector's warden The icter continued " I need not tell you egsin tha I delight la carrying on the duties of the office, for the beat welfare of the church I love no well. NIL Atkin who Las served loyally and well for fourteen years has left the diocese and I have appointed as soy sideatuan tny Mend Mr. J T Locker who I know will give satis- faction both to the parishioners sod my eel!. Of my co-warden I cannot speak too highly." Mr Isherwood nominated Mr Samuel Taylor aa his sidesman.</p>
        <p>• Refore the meeting proceeded with the election of sidemen, Mr R Done said he thought it was desirable in the interests of the church that the vestry should •flioint mcre than eight sidemen. His expertence during the past year was that that number had proved altogether insufficient, and they had lied to call to °M- aid -re to help in the twork.lt would he far more dignified for the Pariah Church to hays officer. eho could he legitimately called upon instead of having to press into their crevice people who had not been elected to office. He moved that the number E. lucreased to twelve. Mr Squire seconded. The Rector said the matter had been lironght before the Chnrch Council, und they had expressed their wish for eight and no more. Mr Isherwood spoke strongly in favonr of maintain- ing the precast arrangement, which he considered find worked very satisfectorily. There had never been the slightent difficulty in getting the offertories chllectod by the officer. out others who assisted, willingly and without prerefire, when called upon. Mr. D ire Enid lie wan sorry the church officers were not often seen collecting at the early morning services. He still maiotained that it was oudifnifted to linve to call upon members of the congregation to assiat the oflicenu.</p>
        <p>G. Te3lor said that if officers did not ettend regularly the vestry Lad the remedy in its oen hands end could appoint other valeta. ' Mr. Donee propositien ma. defeated, culy the proporer and eeconder voting for it. 'the foliowing sidesmen were then elected i—kfeettre R W Collin, It Aattviar. R /larding, 10 U Tsrlor, G Brown, It lee, J 'l' Whittle, I lilt ; Sr. George's klianise, Mr It Sumner ; St. ClervenCe Mission, Mr J II lioughtun. A resoilli ion was parsed placing on record appreciation of the eervices of Meese. Atkin, Jam's Wood and Write.° eho have retired from office after long ant faithful work for the church.</p>
        <p>LA Y REPRE.ArNTATIVF</p>
        <p>Colonel .J D Ittickton and Mr James Wood having relinquished the offices of lay representatives, Messrs Jl' Hopkins, U W A Brown ao.l li Sumner were tiontin- ated as their torcnaor. and the choke of two repre- sentatives will he made at • meeting of parochial electorate April 27th. Mr E A White wee re-elected volley clerk and Messrs PC Price and J T Whittle were re-appointed ituditors.</p>
        <p>II AN,El.l.Oles y IVITATION</p>
        <p>It ens snhouneed that ( honceilor Pepin will hold is sieitition for the ion to office of church- wardens end sidesmen at the Warrington Parish Church on May 4th at 11 a.m. Tilt RECTOR AND TIIE ROLE WEEK AIIS,ION. . . . .</p>
        <p>The Hector, in the course ore brief address, siehl be wished to express Lie very deep and earnest gratitude for the services which had bren rendered to their , church thin year •nd in the past by th• Ctmnainity of I the Resurrection, Mil-field. Ile wanted to speak quite Impartially. The Rev. Eustath Hill (who conduct.° the Holy Week ntission at the Pariah Church) appeared to any one or tyro things with which perhaps all would not agree. He (the Rector) would mention two poinis on which he would wish to differ a little bit. In the first place the Rev. Eustace Hill seemed to take it for granted that St. Mary Magdelene was the woman who was a sinner. SOOic of the Fathers did identity the two and none identified the three women—Mary of Bethany, Mary Magdelene, thd the woman who W/I9 einner—btit he thought tost modern author Ries Thought they Were three distinct persons. Another point on which he dare say some of them felt very deeply was the question of religious doubt. The Rev. Eustis. 11111 seemed to imply that relngious doubt a-ose •Iways from moral fault.. Its (the Rector) did not know that be really did mean that. It was possible and probable that in some Cases religious doubts did erise from moral faiths, hut not sleeps Ile knew many cases in which people had bad most deep and sincere doubts end yet they lived, ea fares he knew, most beautiful lives. Ile bad said what deep gratitude he , I felt to the Girth:unity at klirfield for all they bad done for them in the past. Ibe Rey. Eustthe 11111 preached Claret as few of thorn could preach Him, and one practical result of that wththat the £2O which he (the Rector) bed handed to the people's warden had keen given anonymously ne a thank-offering to God for blesaing received during holy Week. That was perhaps as strong proof es could to given of the benefit of the Euetsce services. Continuing the Rector thlnked the congregation for their Easter offertory. liars pain and grief to him, inn sense, to take it, hot he thanked them very much end he would not take it if he did not feel he needed it. Alluding to the meeting of parochial electors to to held on Thursday rest, the Rector raid it wee of the utmost importance that they should haven, lay representative. devoted Churchmen , who would pledge themselves to attend the conferexces and also be able to speak—nun who loved the church, knew the Faith, and were trying to lire the Christian life. Ile wished to express his gratitude to Colonel Burl:ton and Mr. I James Wood tor their regular attendance at the confer• encee and for the way in which they had spokea for the benefit of the Church. THAN,. _</p>
        <p>Mr. G. Brown, in moving a rote of thanks to the organist and choir, mid that those eh° attended church every Sunday and lietened to the quality of the music and to the precision and pathos with which it um sung recognized that the organist and choir most /Tend a great deal of time, patience and perseverance in prepar- ing such splendid service.. The choir was • voluntary one and was thoroughly deserving of thrinka. 51r. Bog or conded and mid the services at the Parish Church were .5 good as it waa possible to get from • voluntary choir. The Rector said no-one appreciated more deeply than he all that they owed to Mr. Darby and the choir. Everybody must have listened with the greatest ewe. elation to the beautiful music cu Good Friday evening aud Easter Gay.</p>
        <p>The vote was heartily peened. It one announced that Colonel J. D. 800 l ton will shortly leave the district and on the propoeition of Mr. N'. G. Taylor seconded by Mr. S. Taylor the meeting expremef its appreciation of the devoted services he has tendered to the parish for many yews en lay repre• eentative end in other capacitiew The Rector said there wee no branch of Church work in the pariah or in the diocese which Colonel Buckton had not been connected and revved the Church well. Colonel Buckton, in acknowledging the vote, raid he had been connected with the pariah for nearly forty years and for the last thirty years hail had a great dent tt de with Church work. Ile always felt List, nein ought not to undertake public duties unless he was prepared to carry them (hear, hear.)</p>
