        <p>The Enter vestry meeting in connection with the above was held in the vestry on Thursday evening. In the absence of the Vicar, the Hey II It IVild occupied the chair, end among those present were :—Mess.s Jackson and Oldreld (wardens), Sutcliffe (choir master), It Armstrong, Cawley, loft, nu, Taylor, and Forebear (vestry tlerk).—Mr Forshaw mid the state- ment of accounts which was regarded as satisfactory and was panned—Mr Jackson was appointed vicar's warden, anil Mr °Mehl people's warden, whilst the fornier was also sppointed tram:tier. Sir Jackson nominated Mr loft es his warden and Mr Oldtield nominsted Mr Dean.—The vestry appointed, Mrssre J Peen. Ellison, Cawley, Warburton, Armstrong, Taylor. Ilswitt, hlather aril Massey ; Messrs Ashcroft anti Ilorabin were reieppointed auditors. Votes of thanks were passed to the following for their services :—Mr Forebear (clerk), wardims, rides- men, choir end Sunday school teachers, and the Hey. fl. It. Wild for presiding at thin meeting. lIAENABAS'.</p>
        <p>The Easter veers meeting of the above church wits held in the schoolroom on Thiirsdty evening. The ' Vicar (the Rev J llollingworthi presided and amongst those present were :—Meaars E Evans (vicar's warden) and C. Skinner (people's warden).</p>
        <p>Mr Erma road the klatement of account. which showed a balance in hand of El Irs ad. The special offertories hicluded the Easter offering. .C 5 11. 3d ; harvest. £7 5d ; Hospital Sunday, £3 141 41 ; A.C.S. Ell 5s 81. The account. were adopted. 'lke Vicar mid he would like to ray. few words with regard to the past year which Lad been overt happy, harmonlous, and ;dement one. They had men the finishing of one of the schemes of the church extension, namely, the new organ, click had been played for the first tint, last Sunday. Since last October the members of the congregation had received new life and had worked to their utm, st capacity,with the handsome result tint they bed ratted no lem a sum then .ClOO in the flee watts. Ile tbaaked them most heartily twirl when he did nu, it am moat wrongly and feelingly. Mil h the united efforts of all and the bard work on the part crf the church (Allows, they bail obtained a mold praiseworthy r. cult and had duos° signs of htalthy program, upon which be would alwaye look hack with the great, at pride.</p>
        <p>LIFt TI?:•1 pr orric”.7</p>
        <p>TI e Vicar in appointing Mr. J. E. Morgan as Wm warden, raid he was sorry that circumstances had crises which had compelled him to change his warden. It was always sad to part with old friends and he was very sorry to hear that Mr. Esinnie business hnd taken him away to Liverpool. Mr. Banns had been a great help and their relations during his period of orlice had beau most gratifying and friendly. The vestry puesed a hearty vote of thanks to Mr. Lthes for Lis services, to which Mr. liens, in briefly responding said he was glad the little work he hail done had Leen appreciated, nod hoped the little organizations lie had set on foot would be continned.—The vistry appointed Mr. W Skinner ria people's warden.Tbe Vicar vomit atedlleasra W Hewitt end T Oxley Cr sideamen,Mr Skinner ! nominated Messrs ECotterill,llDimelow and W Turner, ' and the vestry appointed Memrs W Boyle, J Hamer, 'l' A Jones, II V Makes!, F Kimberley and J Tomkins. The vestry appointed Messrs C Ekinner and IV Boyle as ' lay repress stalled,</p>
