        <p>There wean very grod sttrrvisoce, iyloteg msny ladies, at the Easter vestry meeting in connection With We church which wee held in the schoolroom on Thursday evening, under the presidency of the Vicar (Rev H II Firth). Arnongst those preeent were the Revs ii H Corebie end E M Swift ant Mr J Fargher Whom the unavoidable &amp;mute of both asidean acted an clerk end submitted the accounts )</p>
        <p>Ili I'. W.A DENS.</p>
        <p>The ViVIT said the annual vewtry meeting was always an important event in any parish and had a legal as well as ecclesiastical value. It wesavory uneful educa- tion for a Churchman to tenon. a warden, ken.,s he got to know a great deal al out the vror king of the church and to recurs that the lay oiNcers bad considerable power end very Important jut roe entrusted to them. There was so good work in ehich the wardens did not take an relive part. 'rho, studied the convenience and comfort of the congrega-</p>
        <p>tion, nod helped the clergy In a thousand ways. It should be impiring to lay nuen to know that the late king Edward VII , when Prince of Wales, acted as churchwarden of Sandringham. Continuing, the Vicar nominated Mr. W. 11. Stanefleld as b.a warden for .nother year, pointing out that he was a most efficient layman who had taken a delight in fillies •Irnost every office that a layman could hold in the Church ot England. He was auperintendent of the Sunday echool, member of the oh tir, a lay reader, secretary or the Church Council, and ai acted as lay representative besides, in big profoesionel capacity, being headmaster of LI at large and important day school. Mr. Eckersley had been people'. warden for ten yearn, and his merits were apparent to everyone who attended the church. I Mr. W. Enke-rale, was re-elected [People's warden. I Mr. Stanslleld bad nominated Mr. Poster as his sides- man and Mr. Eckersley selected Mr. astley. KETINI. OF TOE I ONG lIF.O ATION. The vestry than tesolvi I itself into a meeting of the cons rel Nihon.</p>
        <p>Tbe Vicar expreared his sincere gratitude to the congregation for thiir torndeome gifts to him a the Easter and Ceristmaa offerings. Continuing, he said how plensed the clergy and the church officers were with the way toohich (dud Fliday was observed. They had modes great point of trying to get the day kept in a really suitable manner and he thought that throughout Lire length nod breadth of England people were endeavouring to get that solemn dny el d letter j than it had been in the pest. They knew what splen- did response was mode to the appeal at Sr. James'e. the church being tilled no the morning service. Every chair, a ith the exteption of those in the side chapel, jwas occupied—ln fact, it wen said that the attendence j wan almost like that at a b festival. Having referred to the briratiful se-eicen on Foster tiny, the Vicar said that during the year the church had pursued I the even tenor, of ite ern3s, progress having been j steadily maintained. hey had L3O in hand towards the huildiog of anew St. hilde's mission, rind he hoped that before tie end of the year they would he able to I increane it to £I,OOO. The Vicar watmly thanked Mr. Esirclough for ticking the gardeu adjoining the mission o_att Teti ve,</p>
