      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>CHURCH PROCESSION FROM WESTMINSTER TO ST. PAUL'S.</head>
        <p>CHURCH PROCESSION FROM WESTMINSTER TO ST. PAUL'S.</p>
        <p>A unique and impressive function took place in London on Good Friday, when the Bishop of London attended by his Suffragan Bishops marched from Trafalgar Squire to St. Paul's Cathedral, as" an act of prayer and solemn supplication on the Day of the Atonement in the year in which the representative of the English Church is to be crowned King." There were about 200 clerics and 750 choristers and lay readers all robed in cassock and surplice with fully 2,000 laymen. After the procession bad entered the building a short service was bald. Our picture shows Dr. Ingram giving a blessing from the steps of St. Paul's. •</p>
        <p>London was at the zenith of the hol,day mood on Monday. Four days of uninterrupted sunshine transformed Loudon into c pleasure city The Londoner was early astir, and accompanied by his wife and children directed his steps to the nearest railway station, there to embark upon some adventure of pleasure. Aviation has added a new joy to holiday makers, and the aeronauts who disported themselves at Hendon and Brooklands ctstw large crowds. But the old haunts were not forsaken. Hampstead was as popular as ever. At the Zoo there was •Titrord Md. Ch.rn4o.^•.* crnswi waiting/ thw • ride on the elephants.</p>
