      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>VICTORIA MEMORIAL.</head>
        <p>THE VICTORIA MEMORIAL.</p>
        <p>:Although the unveiling, by the King, of the Quinn Victoria Memor;al will not lake place until the middle of next month, everything is now in readiness for the ceremony. The scaffolding and wooden hoarding has been removed during the last few days from the centre piece, and now, for the first time, the public are able to view the magnificent group of statuary. The Statue shown in the picture is 16 ft. 610. in height, and is one of the three groups representing " Justice,- "Truth ". and "Motherhood,- the latter is seen on the left. All these figures and the Ware ',liar A, 0111 Si whhh they rest are, likc the base, la Carrara marble.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>CHILDREN'S CORNER, WAIMEA by UNCLE CALEB.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER, WAIMEA by UNCLE CALEB.</p>
        <p>THE LAZY WINDMILL A TALE WITH A MEANING.</p>
        <p>Once upon a time there was a big old . wind- mill. It belonged to a farmer. _ . . _</p>
        <p>It had worked hard for many,roany years, and the farmer, when speaking of it, used to call it "Old Faithful."</p>
        <p>But one day the windmill had stopped working, _</p>
        <p>The Wheel stood perfectly still. A strong ' wind passed by. It said to the windmill, "Come, come! Why are you not at work? Let me help you turn your wheel." "No, no!', said the windmill, "I do not wish for your help. I shall rest all day to-day. I am so tired!" "Oh, but we must always work before we rest," said the wind. "Come, I shall be glad to help you." The wind blew upon the wheel. The wheel would not turn. "I will try again," said the wind. So it blew long and hard.</p>
        <p>._..„ "I do not have to mind you!" said the windmill. "I tell you lam tired, and I shall rest to-day: .Go away, old wind !"</p>
        <p>So the wind sighed and went away. "Oh, how fine it is to rest !" said the windmill.</p>
        <p>All day long it remained still. It grew late and the tired horses eame home. They had worked worked hard all day in the hot sun.</p>
        <p>itIM-Y-W—ei;;;J-thirsty. jWhen they saw the windmill, they started into a little trot, in their haste to reach the water-tank.</p>
        <p>Poor, tired horses! It was empty. "Is the windmill broken 1 " asked the 'irmer.</p>
        <p>:1,4.?: it is all right."</p>
        <p>...,_ "Why hasn't it worked to-day? The wind blew hard, too. I can't understand this!" he said.</p>
        <p>The cows came for a drink. The sheep came for a drink. The chickens came for a drink. The dog came for a drink. The birds came for a drink. There was no water. "Oh, why didn't I work?' said the windmill. "There is no water for the horses and I cows!</p>
        <p>"There is no water for the sheep and chickens I</p>
        <p>"There is no water for the dog and the birds!</p>
        <p>"Oh, I am so sorry I didn't work!" And the windmill began to creak and groan in sorrow.</p>
        <p>"Oh, I wish the wind would blow! "I would work so hard that the tank would soon be full!" A gentle wind came. It tried to turn the wheel, but it was too weak; so it called its friends.</p>
        <p>The friends came. They all pushed and pushed I Suddenly the wheel stopped creak- ing and groaning, and as it whirled merrily around, it sang this song</p>
        <p>" Wirh the wiedTo hip me, Round and round I'll go go Then the sparkling water To my tank will Hon."</p>
        <p>Soon the tank was full of clear, cold, sparkling water, and, as the animals eagerly drank their fill, the wheel busily turned round and round, humming quite happily to itself.</p>
        <p>THIS WEEK'S STORY. A BAD JUDGE. An Irishman, taking home a goose for his' Sunday dinner, called at an inn for refresh- ment. Laying down the bird, he was pro- ceeding to satisfy his thirst when la seedy- looking person, seizing the goose made off. Pat at once gave chase, and soon had the man by the neck. "What did yez take that burrd for ?" asked the Irishman. "Oh," said the seedy-looking individual, "I took it for a lark ?" "Did yez," said Pat. "Shore, ye'd make a bad judge at a poultry show." GOLDEN KEYS.</p>
        <p>A I noel. Of golden keye is mine To make each day ividi gladness shine.</p>
        <p>"flood morning," tha.'s the golden key That unlocks every door for me.</p>
        <p>When evening comes, "Good-night," I say, And clam the door of each glad day. When at the table, "If you please' I take from off my bunch of keys.</p>
        <p>When friends give anything to. me, I'll use a little •' 'rhank you " key.</p>
        <p>"Excuse me, beg your pardon," too. When by mistake some harm I do. Or if unkindly harm is given, With" Forgive me " I shall be forgiven. On a golden ring these keys I'll bind ; This in its motto "Be ye kind."</p>
        <p>BERN! A. —The new Fluid Psd Tress sever gets hard rind is effectual in most difficult cases For slight cases the new IVaterproof Washable 'from, 'which is always clean and moat durable, is strongly recommended.— WEITZ &amp; WRIGHT, Surgical Instrument makers, 93, Banshee at. opposite topof Bold at., Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>riiiIIINGI CHA11,,,, j,R,SA WARRINGTON A CENTURY AGO.</head>
        <p>riiiIIINGI CHA11,,,, j,R,SA WARRINGTON A CENTURY AGO.</p>
        <p>A CHAPTER OF INTERESTING REMINISCENCES.</p>
        <p>The horn' of our youth is a perennial subject for those who are priveleged to grow old, and seldom fails to set free the tongue and memory. The chapter which follows is copied from a manuscript written by a War- ringtonian who was born in October 1799 and whose early memories take us back to the period of a century ago. The references to the Stocks and Pillory are decidedly interest- ing and we shall hope to tell something more concerning these public punishments another day., . . _</p>
        <p>- We shall leave to our readers the task of guessing the sex and identity of the writer. A.M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL.</head>
        <p>CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>I attended for four years the "old " other- wise the "Presbyterian "Chapel and also the Sunday school therewith connected. At that time the school was held in the chapel under- neath the gallery, the tuition was adminis- tered on the Lancastrian plan, and I was appointed a monitor. The school inspector was Mr. Holbrook (brother to Messrs. Samuel John and William) Gaskell, who 8180 lectured and preached occasionally. He was a " poldavy master" and had a large number of weavers in and around Warrington. He was very wealthy, and at death left a very large fortune. The Rev. Thomas Broadbent was pastor of the chapel. His sermons doctrinally considered were to a Trinitarian, vague and unsatisfactory. His eon Thomas, taught myself and a companion, E.G., after school hours were over, for which purpose we sat in a pew in the chapel. We were also members of the voluntary choir, which consisted of about a dozen young women. On entering the singing loft, a gallery, on the Sabbath, we doffed our bonnets and sus- pended them on pegs placed in the wall to receive them. The only instrumental music we possessed emanated from a fiddle and a brass viol. At that period the congregation was limited in number and on some occasions we 'have counted the persons present, and found them to be only a dozen, and those composing families related to one another. But though small in numerical strength, yet most of the ' members of the congregation were very wealthy people, and jointly supported the Sunday school, and other incidental objects and associations, without appealing to the public, or resorting to anniversary collections in the chapel. Master Thomas Broadbent, though ) influenced by religious feeling, was remark- able for his buoyant disposition. Sometimes ) a girl has been stationed at one of the Chapel windows, watching for his father's appear- ance, whilst he has been humming over for us his mother's favourite air "Oh dear what ) can the matter ba ! " He was sent to college, and returned about the close of the year 1811, dressed in knee-breeches and black silk stockings, when from his altered demeanour, we felt that we could not make so free with him as formerly. Subsequently he called us to him, and questioning us touching our 1 ) belief in God and Heaven, the Devil and Hell,and finding us orthodox Trinitarians he told us that they had erased Satan, devil, hell, etc., out of our hymn books, which would shortly be replaced by new ones. My feelings were so hurt that I never went</p>
        <p>Mr. Broadbent junior, who succeeded to the pastorate on the death of his father, lived in Latchford, and died very suddenly one Saturday night, after composing a sermon in praise of Queen Caroline.</p>
        <p>THE STOCKS.</p>
        <p>The Stocks about 1808 were located at the Bridge Foot. I remember seeing a Sabbath inebriate, with his feet confined therein one Sunday morning as I went to Divine service. He had plenty of associates or company round him, who talked and laughed and joked as if the affair had been intended to do him honour, rather than as a disgraceful punish- ment.</p>
        <p>Apparently at some former period the stocks must have been portable, or at least removable, for I have heard that on one occasion some comrades of a prisoner got the stocks loose, and drew them and the incarcerated prisoner through the town, and had a " jovial " day's fun, frolic and drink- ing. Where Paul Caldwell was during this spree, was a mystery, as he was sharp, clever, and the terror of everybody, whether good or bad, at least so far as youngsters were concerned. But perhaps it occurred before Paul's accession to office. THE PILLORY.</p>
        <p>The Pillory was situated in the Corn Market,but was a far less genial instrument. No sympathy for the poor prisoner here. No friendly chatting with the b:-standers, whereby the flight of time was accelerated. No, the Pillory was looked upon as a real instrument of punishment, and not a mere sham. Whoever was confined within its dreaded embraces, was believed to be verit- ably and indeed guilty, and little mercy had to be expected from the loungers and frequenters of the market. Whenever the Pillory was used, Mrs.</p>
        <p>Wells, teacher of a sewing school (and mother of the Rev. George Henry Wella,M.A. ,of the Gorton Unitarian Chapel), in order to</p>
        <p>„ impress her pupils with a just abhorrence of crime, and to give them a just conception of the punishment that awaited it, gave us a holiday for the purpose of witnessing the event. This was about 1811. The criminal's head and hands were pro- jected through the loopholes, and the machine was circulated by a cord in the con- trol of the custodian. The populace enjoyed, in every sense of the word, the privilege which they had of throwing rotten eggs and other soft substances against the prisoner's face, but stones were strictly prohibited, although, it would seem sometimes at least in vain, as the human target was frequently besmeared with gore before the punishment came to its conclusion. "RIDING THE STANO.” .</p>
        <p>Husband beaters were honoured with " stang riding," an extrajudicial punishment, inflicted by the populace, who acted as public canons in the absence of any legislation, either national or local, taking cognizance of those unseemly domestic drubbinge. The exhibitions, which were far from uncommon, were usually on this wise. The "loving" couple were represented by a man and a woman riding on a large horse, generally high in bone and low in flesh. The real woman had donned the breeches and other etceteras of male attire, whilst the man, poor, subservient, hen-pecked fool was arranged in feminine garments, with a huge poke-shaped bonnet, and holding in his hands, as emblems of submission, a mop and a long brush.</p>
        <p>His cap, adapted from the current style, did not sit well, and was rqmarkable for as. elevated crown, unfortunately concealed by the bonnet during the procession, and for enormous borders, which flapped in his face ' most unpleasantly. Of course a motley crowd of men, women and children, joined in the carnival, and shouted and hooted and 3ielled as hard and as fast as their stentorian lungs would permit. Not a few had old tin cans, old kettles, and old pans or pots, from which they contrived, by means of sticks and staves, to wring some most exquisite harmony. A speech or " nonning," adapted to the particular occasion, was chanted or recited, of which the burden was— Itsn-a-d.n-dan '</p>
        <p>Little Nan Lathan bee banged her god ran.</p>
        <p>The name is no fiction, bui who this Amazon was, or for what reason she drubbed her husband we know not.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>BUTTER MARKET.</head>
        <p>THE BUTTER MARKET.</p>
        <p>The Butter Market at the above period was attended by about one hundred bona fide farmers daughters, who stood in two rows of nearly equal numbers. Twenty ounces to the pound was considered the legal standard weight in those days, and the market lookers ' and weighers went round selecting the 'cast promising lump in each basket and testing its weight. On one occasion just as the triers were about to inspect a certain basket a wide- awake bystander, observed its owner insert a half crown into a lump which she was afraid would not be competent to stand the test. Going up to the woman he inquired her price per pound, tendered her the money, and insisted on having that particular lump. She offered him another and better looking fresher piece of butter, etc.. but he remained firm, and as the triers were now upon her, to get rid of his threats she most ram tantly handed him his choice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>STORY OF A DREADFUL SECRET.</head>
        <p>THE STORY OF A DREADFUL SECRET.</p>
        <p>Willi an account of a Warrington Free- mason and his impenetrable secret, we will close these reminiscences. One of the mem- bers of this mysterious secret society, had a most inquisitive janging wife, who was ever and anon importuning her 1i ge lord to com- municate to her the "freemason secret." One day,after divers precautionary measures, he exacted a solemn promise of secrecy, and then proceeded to divulge what had long lain on his breast, and blighted his present happiness and future prospects..</p>
        <p>He then detailed how he had slain a Scotch - man with a pack on his back (a species of murder then epidemic) and buried him ir. a box in a certain lonely)spot. Shortly after, while gossiping with a neighbouring crony, his spouse enjoining strict secrecy, gradually told her all about the murder and interment. With similar entailed injunctions. the murder, or at least the account of it, passed through the mouths and distorting and inventive brains of all the old gossips in the locality. . . . . . . .</p>
        <p>Having. lost nothing -in transmission but rather improving in its passages, the atrocity came to the ears of the authorities, who ordered the fellow into custody forthwith. On his appearance before the magistrates, the poor man related everything very straightforwardly and accompanied by an officer, pointed out the spot, where they soon disinterred a wooden box, which contained a snail with its horns on its hack. The magis- trates perceiving the drift of his ruse, set him at liberty, when he emigrated to a foreign land, and it was said, never again returned either to his wife or to Warrington.</p>
      </div>
