      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>MR. A. J. KING AND PEACE.</head>
        <p>MR. A. J. KING AND PEACE.</p>
        <p>Hopeful speeches were delivered at the annual meet- ing oo Wednesday evening, of the Liverpool Peace Society. The Principal speaker was Mr. heir Hardie, M.P., who mitised lb. Biebrop of Liverpool'. recent sermon to the Territorials, and complained that the Church°, Cbrlat was not standing out melon militarism se it ought. Resolutions were missed in favour of arbitration instead of war, and welcoming the propmed treaty . ith the United Staters. Mr. Alfred J. King,(m.M.P. for Knutsford Division) moved :—" that this meeting cordially welcomes the proposal made by the President of the United States of America for, treaty of arbitration to cover all difference., without exception, that may arise between the two nations, and rejoice. at the cordial reception which it bas met with from all pities throughout the country." lie said, that whilst they regretted that professedly Christian people bad tot applied themsol•ea with sufficient energy to the canoe of peace and arbitra- tion, end they had had to suit for other force., economic pressures and no on, they yet welcomed their help, and auy help which would bring forward the day to which they all no greatly looked forward. The increneed oat of armamentu in times of pace was becoming almost intolerable, and was a menace to civilisation ands disgrace to our statesmanship. Mr George Rom seconded, and the motion we, carried,</p>
      </div>
