      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>IS THE Itutl.)-(10(1 EXTINCT?</p>
        <p>in last week's Muer there wan. natural expression of satisfaction that the driver of the taxi-csrh which killed a Penketh child was not blameworthy, but it was added that the populons village in question suffered many things of many recites" motorist., and this habitual disregard of the frot-pasungerte elemen- tary tights wan strongly deprecated. The comment was evidently penned unit, great eelf-re aaaa int and it is high tinie ihat inch protests increased and multiplied. Let then, be mild and dignified, if you will, but they should be general awl they should be permitted in until they have the desired effect. Lot me net in sharp contrast examples if Opposite extremes in this respect. At the lart annual dinner of the Northern Section of the Automobile Associa- tion and Motor Union in Manchester, Lord Immobile took an altogether toi sanguine view of the situation. S.id he: When mot- ring began motorists were dencribed as road-hope. In those days there were many pimple who got into a motor, put on a pair of goggles, and entirely i.rpoi there were people who had an much right to the King's high road aa they Ind themselves, lie was glad to say that was • thing if the past. That thin was premattire,to say the least, DI edit no proof, and I may proceed to my further illuOration without comment. " The motor-car is a rec.-lode, d institution in thin country. It is all the more necessary, then fore, to control it. vagaries The railway companies on their own lande are heavily mulcted for any accident because they can be brought to book," alp, fin "Brood Au row --The Naval and Military Gazette." "The motor fiend mho' through lands soil over r, ad. for which he pays little or nothing, kills humeri beings aid animals, and, anises his number can be taken, drives off no t-free. Not only does he amuse himself at the risk of life, but he even pays myrmidons to enable him to evade the police. This is had enough on the great high roads, but side- roads, with no for vpathe, are almost equally at the mercy of these nelfish Itinerant rate- , payers sun forced to take refuge in the hedges aid ditches whilst motors aweep gaily by. The death- roll aloud by these infernal macbinm is too high to pant linnet keit" All and anndry may be assured that, for pleasure as well as for commercial purpose., the motor vehicle ban come to rtay, but while we mly not have it otherwise, and would net if we coeld, the road-I,g, which unforionstely in not as the dab", must be ecotched. To merely sound a disecridant • hooter while duhiog at unreels speed along the high- way in not" good enough." The chauffeur who does that, even with the authority of a rich employer, is not entitled to exoneration from punishment or , emu, on the plea when, having put no check on his excessive speed but relitd on the road being • cleared, he kill.r r maim, a heedless juvenile or an ! adult whir for aught he knows may be • deed mute. ' To concede the implied claim to priority of selfish and thoughtless motorist, would be to reduce the ' public highway to the condition of • level crossing r on the railway. Urea. d that the road-bogs, while certrinly far from an extinct specie., ern in • decided minority ;anton. hose who know how to behave though motoring, In their enamel Imm dincover and stamp out tie animal in goestion, the spirt in ! hich they delight vill everywhere be brought inlo disrepute, and the gentlemanly among them will come under the general condemnation. TOASTS.</p>
        <p>The re-productine in last week's" Examiner " of Mrs. Carry Nat,on'e toast reminded me of the first tm record no far ea these i-lands are coreerned. Here, in brief, is the story : -When Ilengiet the Baton brooght his beautiful daughter Rowena to Kent the was intrAuced to the /kith') chief N'ortigern at • banquet. Modestly advancing towaida the King, according to the cuetom of her country, she held out a golden cup of mead, "Wars bail, hlsfor Coney." (" Health to my lord"), she said. However, the "Tatter" ban the following as to the otigin of the word teferted to -"It was an old English custom to put a toast —a roasted pippin or so- in a hot chink, suck as a tankard of ale or each. Many if the wits of the last age s ill arisen that it (the name of the treats) had its rite from an accident at Bath in the reign of Charles 11. It happened that on a public day a celebrated beauty was in the Crone Bath, std one of the crowd of her mlinirers took a glass of the water in which the f..ir one stood, atil drank her health to the emit any. There was in the phice a gay fellow half- fuddled, who tlTered to jump in, and •wore that though be lilt", not the liquor he would have the toast. This whim gave fonntlation to the present honour which in done to the lady we mention in our liquors, who has ever since been called • toast." I have a fairly good collection of manta, meetly humorous, and when I have a convenient 'rumen I may draw uron thew. LORD fit/SCHEN.</p>
        <p>The puldication of the life of this Gladstonian statesman brings to my mind the fact that rather more than a score years ago I interviewed him for a West Yorkshire paper, and wrote my account in the local dialect as if the conversation had token place between an old Yorkshire. Radical and the eminent politician. His lordship was the guest of the late Sir Alfred Arnold at the time, and that gentleman sent him a copy of the paper containing the interview. Lord Goschen took it all in good part, in fact, he Inn to good as to say some rather flattering things about the report—though whether he really understood a word of it may he well doubted. LA MILO.</p>
        <p>From the decayed Lord just mentioned In the very much alive though frilly statumume La Mio, who in this week at the hippodrome here, in indeed a far try ; but I may just remark that I interviewed thin decidedly irteresting lady in East Lancashire two or three years ago, and that in the meantime much has happened to tdd to her attractions as a public enter- tainer. Some towns would have none of her, but I could never underntantl why, and I fancy that Warringtonana visiting the Palace this week will share my sentiments of admiration and ,omniendation. BRONTE-LAND.</p>
        <p>I spent pail of the Enter holidays sauntering *boot the Brent.° country, and in my rambles fell in with Mr. Bradley, the Stanbury Behr. olmaster. What Mr. Bradley does not know •Inout Haworth parish, the Brontes, and the old folk of thaw parts is not worth knowing, and be is away, ready to give of his inexhaustible ntore. Be is cal ital compary, and it is a great privilege to spend an hour or two with trim. Ile lover the morns, and knows •Il about the flora and fauna of the region round shout, and his enthorimm and devotion have led him no to train his banters that most of them are genuine and intelligent nature-losers, too. Instead of spending their leisure in mischief and rtenselesa . .</p>
        <p>• demi-notion, they ranible in seerch of rare botanical, entomological and ornithological specimen., and the effects are wholesome and beneficial all round. The echrolmnster 0 a good photographer, nod gives lantern lecture,. A BIBULOUS UMBRELLA.</p>
