        <head>OUR SERIAL STORY.</head>
        <p>OUR SERIAL STORY.</p>
        <p>VAL Mourn REsr.LrEn)</p>
        <p>A MYSTERY OF YAIFAIR„</p>
        <p>JOHN STRANGE WINTER.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XX.</p>
        <p>• STORMY INTZRYIZW.</p>
        <p>"Blacken Place, Camden Town," said Rodrigues to the driver of a hansom, and he lumped in, dropped back on the cushions, weary but yet excited. He was on his way to pay a surprise 'deli to the nefarious Mr. Ni. "Faster," he said through the flap to the driver. They rattled and jolted along Oxford-street, turned the corner into Totten. ham•rourt-road, and here Rodriguez en- deavoured to drown kin thoughts by taking a languid interest in the endless procession of vehicles which thronged the roadway. For weeks he had been a dullard, with no life, no anything to rouse his restless spirit. He was now on the more, with funds at his di,. posal, and he wits terribly in earnest in his desire to obtain the necklace, the chart, and the miniature. Miriam was the pivot of a plot, and the centre of a mpitery which was not very clear to him. "Truth is a valuable asset, but deceit hat Its uses. Mix the two with sufficient skill, and the combination would belle even an as- tute man or woman of the world."</p>
        <p>The more he dwelt upon Miriam's desire to regain the miming jewels and document the more he became convinced that his policy wan to retain them in his possession 'should he succeed in the undertaking which he had pro- mised to carry out. Evidently they were clues or proofs; but to what? Their precise value or meaning was nun noweven ine most Important thing. Ile meant to seenre them, but having done no he fully intended to hold them in his own hand an hostages to for- tune, and by means of them to place himmelf on an equal footing with Miriam. At m- eant she held him fast, bound hand and foot as it were, and could by a word place his neck in a fatal noose, a dreary outlook for a man who loved life and its pleasures to the very full. Ile frowned darkly as he remem- bered that at the present moment he was ab- solutely at the mercy of a thoroughly un- scrupulous woman. Ills credit in practieally all parts of the world was exhausted, his debts were for him enormous, but OA then these did not trouble him very much for he knew that he was safe enough at Marlow, and that troublesome duns and writs could not reach him there.</p>
        <p>For a while his safety was assured, but the inflexible fingers of the criminal law might clutch him at any moment, and when he least expected to be canght. Miriam's wealth alone could make the future eafe. She was the frail vessel to which he wan clinging, and if she flung him overboard he would be en- gulphed in a sea of poverty and starva- tion, sinking among the dregs of the sub- merged, never to rise again. Should he then miserably perish for want of a little nerve and trouble? No, a thousand times no. He determined that he would never tempt provi- dence by biting the hand that fed him, he would be faithful to the woman who had vowed that very morning to be his wife. His sensual nature craved for the luxuries with which she supplied him, while poverty he ab- horred, ile was on the brink of ruin, and must save himself from falling into the gulf of wreteliednesa and actual want. He was in the realms of conjecture when he thought of implacable fate in the person of Simeon, and huddled far bark in thy -71, in a cold sweat of fear lest some unforeseen accident might fling him in the path of the French detective. But Rodrigues was not of the breed to fall into a panic at close danger, it was the un- certain distance that quenched his courage. The aroma of an Espanola and the steady rythni of the traflic lulled his fears to rest and calmed his nerves, the unseen terrnr with which Simeon inspired him became less and less pronounced till it faded away altogether. He screened his features behinii a paper, though he trusted his face, now clean shaven, would not be recognised by frienda or enemies.</p>
        <p>There was a stumble and a clatter, and the horse was jerked by the driver to a stand- still. Rodrigues alighted, paid his fare, walked to the end of the street, and waited till the cab had been driven round the corner into the Hampstead-road. Blacken-place is a narrow thoroughfare cn very small villas. The front gardens were gay ith flowers, and the tiled paths neat and Cleanly swept. The lace curtains hanging at the little bay windows gave the jerry-built residences a mo.ttat show of respectability. By this time tie aftei noon had waned, and dusk was fast deepening into night, the pave- ment cleared of children and their mothers. Re went quietly towards the villa which bore the number 27, when a tradesman's cart dashed by. Jermyn•street was painted on the panel thereof, and he recollected in a Ila.h that his rent was unpaid, and that the luggage he had left behind him must remain intact, or the incriminating evidence secreted In his trunks would, perchance, be brought to light. A post-office was close by, and illio the shop he walked, purchased paper and an en.elope, borrowed pen and ink, and wrote a short note of instructions to his landlord that he should lock up his mom, and take special care of his luggage. Enclosing a flee. pound note, be registered and delivered the directed envelope to the girl behind the coun- ter. He stood undecided at the iron gate of No. 27, debating with himself whether he shoeld knock or leave his visit till a later boor. The measured tread of an advancing policeman, however, brought his indecision to an end, and he strode up the tiled path- way and rang the bell, which was Instantly answered by a neatsmaill-ser.vaiit.</p>
        <p>"Your business, sir?" said she. Rodrigues was about to reply, when a cough from &amp;bole caused him to stop. He comprehended the significance of the cough, and, glancing upwards, he saw the reboubt. able Mr. Niggine leaning over the banniAter. . . . .</p>
        <p>"Bring the doctor 'ere., Mary," he called. Rodriguez was shown his wan up the stairs and into a room at the top of the landing. No sooner was the s.rvant gone than Nigeins bolted the door, an uncomfortable grin on his km He demanded, in a menacing tone, 'What's yer game, a-romin"ere in a re- apectable taspaver's 'miser 'You received my wire." " I ain't got no wire." "" •- "</p>
        <p>"A telegriim." "Yee ain't Blrd if ye! are. Why, so yer is ! The Marlow toff, his 'air gone— shaved clean as a babby's 'end. Well-1— am ._jiggered."g rinez's keen eyes were on the man, and Niggins shuffled to and fro like a bear in a cage. He had a wholesome fear of hi, visitor, and a guilty conscience told him that be was in a particularly tight corner. " Where's the girl?'' Rodrigues asked, ctTit and to the point. In the kitchen."</p>
        <p>"Where are the diamonds?" demanded Rodriguez sternly. "Where's the wet?" answered Niggins, in yearee alaatonishment at the question. rh necklace you changed, you infernal scoundrel." hissed Rodriguez savagely. 'Yer 'ad 'em tins blessed mornin', he began, but the other's sneering smile told him that something had happened, of which he wig not aware.</p>
        <p>"S'welp me Onwd, I'm atrite," ex. chimed..</p>
        <p>'•Niggina, you are a liar." "Yet got the ease in rer 'and." .Traah!" throwing the imitation stones Violently on the mule. "Them 'ere—" -•• • - - - •</p>
        <p>"Wie-re. the real ones!" cried Rodri• rTrimnstnndittngytr.airoll: ' Nggrn ato stick that .ere knife o' vourn into me, is yen? So rip me, I'm • par, cony." he snuffled lamely. paid you three hundred 10.00.10 for them," said Rodriguez. clenching hi,</p>
        <p>Lrooroo COMPOUND' for Cob., Colds, and Brow*lral troubled!. RICO&amp; lid. Of Cbemista.—k,.</p>
        <p>"Yet' did rialto hornet, an' there's the vally. What'z the sport, me Ind?" " Where are the real ones" Rodriguez persisted in a tone of concentrated fury. "I say, guv'nor, yer ain't 'ad much sleep, per mighty white about the gills." Rodriguez's face was pale, there was a haggard look of care, a darkness under the eyes that told the tale of what he had been through during the past few hours. "Take care," said he, threateningly, "or your diet will be dry bread and gruel, • plank bed and an iron cubicle." "It ain't had as on that, I ain't copped yet." In spite of his pretended bravado the rogue's teeth were chattering with fear, for imprisonment was to him an ever present terror. . _ .</p>
        <p>"Mr. 14 iggins, I do not permit a man to swindle me with impunity; if he succeeds in doing it, lie bas to pay the price. Those none reigns, obtained by false pretences, must be refunded. ' '"</p>
        <p>"Nar, do' yer see green in these 'ere peepern?" "No prevarication, yon scoundrel." Rod- riguez's voice was cold am? grating. " Rif ht y'are mate," blustered the re. eciver. "This is a grave matter, Mr. Niggles." "So long as there ain't a sexton in the job, I don't mind," rejoined the rogue, trying game of bluff. "We'll waive the money question." "00, will we? Nort me. Them quids ain't • gain' to be waved, they are safe in the bank and there, my noble toff, they remains, till they sprouts into thousands. You an' I 'ate got to square that knifin' business, xer known" . .</p>
        <p>" I'll throttle you." "No fear. Take per 'rind from my coffer," expostulated Niggins, wriggling from the other'a nervous fingers. "flora did you obtain the real diamonds?" Rodi inn°, demanded. "They were nicked. I means I bought em in a regular way o' biz." "Did you know from whence then were stolen."</p>
        <p>"Nar, not till yew tells me." "Do you let lodgings?" asked Rodriguez, abruptly. " Do yer think pore old Niggins is by him- self, an stick a knife in Ito?" said the re- ceiver, fumbling at the of the door in order to effect his escape. - "Don't be an idiot, I'll do you no harm, Only give me the information I am seeking. You were the tenant of a house in the Seven Dials and the rooms were let to a class often wanted by the police." Niggins turned savagely on Ids unwelcome visitor. . .</p>
        <p>'Wort dyer mean castin' slurs on my lodgers? One on 'em was a regular tip-topper, the Honourable Alec Blandford." . .</p>
        <p>"Lord Illandford'a non! You are lying, you scoundrel," exclaimed Rodriguez. "Gospel, I tell yer. 'E's • pal o' mine, called on 'im in Jerymn-etreet, an 'e treated me jig proper. Tipple ! Slap up—an' el gors, primo." Niggins swaggered to the opposite side of the room, and the other asked un- " What became of the man on the stairs?" . .</p>
        <p>Niggins' ferrety eyes closed into cat-like slits.</p>
        <p>"What the deuce do yeW know about the man chucked on my lan;lin', and no boots on 'is pore feet, • gash on 'is pore 'end big enough to post a letter in. Ye're a nice sort o' cove to be in London, bashing people's 'edits and stiekin' knives into 'em—'ere stow that," he yelled, when Rodrigues suddenly threw a heavy book at him, striking him full in the chest. .</p>
        <p>" Where ht that man nowt" Rodrigues asked, endeavouring to eoneeal his anxiety under an assumed indifference.</p>
        <p>" If I splits, we'll say nothin' &amp;bold them quids?" " Agreed, provided you tell me the truth." "'Es Hein' in Jermyn street, along o' the Kidder."</p>
        <p>... "The Kidder!" echoed Rodriglies, bewil- dered. "The Honourable Alec Blandford, the big- gest doer what ever toed a line. I'm trond on "But I don't understand?" Nigginm, relieved of him permonar rearm, laughed till the tears rolled down his elieekm.</p>
        <p>"It was the Kidder an snapped the di- manila," lie shrieked. " You tell me 13Iandford is a thief !" he asked incredulously. " The night I came by the diamonds," said Wiggins, " the peelers was arter use, an' I i slipped the case n 'is pocket. I did a scoot. gave the cops leg bail, an' left 'im my banker. The artful coon got on the scent, did a swap, an' there we is, 'e sold me, gospel—an stuck to the sparklers as is worth a pot." 'Tenth" said Rodrigues, his firm hand grasping Wiggins coat. " Tek my bible hoath." . . . . .</p>
        <p>" And in which house in Jermyn-street does the Honourable Alec Blandford live?" Rodriguez inquired. "Four doors to the right o' yours." " First floor?" Niggins nodded. " Then it woe into his room that I rushed by accident." ' You'd been a doin' a prig?" suggested Niggins Cu an esctacy of enjoyment. " No, no," said the other impatiently. for the association with the fence wan sicken- ing him. "I most obtain an interview. How, when, where? I must not be seen by Lawson, that would mean extinction," he exclaimed, strik- ing his hands together in an agony of doubt. " Yer ain'ter • goin'? No use. E'er sailed the stmt." " France?" " Parris."</p>
        <p>"I am going. and shall want your help." " What me, me tossed in the briny, a mixin' breakfast, dinner, an' supper in my stomach, not for Niggins---" And the fence rolled back in his chair, his face chalky white at the mere recollection of a stormy sea voyage he had once experienced when crossing to Antwerp to dispose of several packets of diamonds, the proceeds of a bur- glary in Hatton-garden. "You are sure that he has gone to Paris?" asked Rodriguez thoughtftigy. " Dead cert. The cook an' me is a courtin', 'ard at work a savin' life diba to make a 'appy 'ome. She give me the infor- mation." Niggius leered and chuckled, and Rodriguez felt a sudden desire to strangle the grinning brute on the spot. "If per after the necklet es took it to gay Parree, yew bet the artful kidder knows</p>
        <p>"has. he gone by himself?" " Nor. Three chaps beeides." " Who are they?' "Can't say. Didn't spot 'em." "Was one of the four short, stout, and " A Pa rlce-voo, 'e jist was, an' • heye that fair funked me. I did a slope when 'e twig- ged me at the stition." "Simeon !" muttered Itodrigties. "Unbolt the door."</p>
        <p>Wigginswilling hands wee drawing the bolt, but the otror s sinewy fingers gripping his wrist prevented the door from opening. "Remember an old adage. A still tongue makes a wise head. I pay a bander them," said Rodriguez, producing the horn handle from which a thin glittering blade darted forth.</p>
        <p>liggne shook. bin arms relaxed, tit, blood receding from his cheeks and lips, leaving his fare the pallid hue of a corpse. Ile mum- bled faintly, his mouth opening in a spas- modic effort to speak. Rodrigues looked hard at Niggins. A skil- ful movement of the wrist and the shining stiletto with the broken point was pressing against the throat of the terrified fence, who dropped a limp heap in the corner of thr room. The other, seeing the t ho httl created a lasting impression, closed the door, running lightly down the steep flight of narrow stairs and out into the street. A cloud was gathering about him, but he was reckless. Was he about to un- earth a secret that had laid hidden lot yearn? Ha knew not. Nothing was certain, save that he wal.bonad to; .</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXI. 81/LION'S STRANOI ADVIINTURI.</p>
        <p>Contiguous to the Avenue de L'opera, there is an unpretentious chop whoa° window is etrongly guarded by a grill of brass wire work; behind this barrier of safety, gems of fabulous price lie in their white satin cases. A bell rings continuously when the door lead- ing from the boulevard is opened, and in an- swer to the numinous there iSSUCS from behind a glass screen at the end of the railed-in counter an immaculately dressed man of a type only to be seen in the more select quar- ters of Paris. The courtly bow and the de. licately chosen words are indications that his dealings are among the favoured ones of the earth. Chance customers are few and far be, twcen, but the bulky ledgers in the safes of Memieurs Georges l'arlotte et Cie record large transaetionn among the most powerful personages in Europe. taut sunlit are here advanced on family heirlooms and no whisper of the transactions is ever heard beyond the portals of the premises. At an oblong table in the private room of the principal sat two men absorbed in con- versation. 't he white hair of one was thick and bushy, his up-turned moustache bustled fiercely while speaking, and there was a burn- ing glow in the solitary eye that he poskessed which seemed to probe beneath the impene- trable mask of indifference worn by the other, who was leisurely unknotting the red tape with which was boned a card-board box wrapped in thick brown paper. The lid of the box opened, a layer of wool removed, and there lay the neeklace, which was leading to results undreamed of even by the sagacious and experienced detective, Simeon, who good leaning over the table, the string of diamonds sparkling in his hand. "Do you recognise this ornament, lion- sienr Parlotte?" . .</p>
        <p>The gentleman addressed • balanced his monocle on his little finger, tapped his none, laughed softly and pulled a red curtain our the top half of the glazed door. Thin was a signal they were not to he disturbed. He spoke no though fearing to be overheard. " Have I seen this necklace, do you ask? My books, to which I alone have access, marks the date when it wan safe in our strong room. Yen, Monsieur Simeon, I know it and also its painful history." He rained his finger that he should not be interrupted. "flat, Monsieur, though I have locked in my breast the history of those gems, I cannot betray the confidence reposed in an by those who honour our firm by their patronage. My mouth is sealed even to you." "To the secret police?" " Yes, even to the secret police I should he silent. My books are in cypher, the key to which I carry in my brain." Simeon hesitated for • minute, he was act- ing unofficially and he desired to obtain the diamond merchant's aid. Parlotte went on speaking. • "I warn you that I cannot open those led. I gun and reveal their contents unless you disclose in strict confidence the channel through which those historic diamonds came into your possession." . . . . .. •</p>
        <p>The' deteCtive took time to consider, he woo in that room for the purpose of obtaining not imparting information. In dividing his know- ledge, he was cogniennt he had nought to fear, Monsieur Parlotte's reputation for pro- bity being well known to the police. "Confidence for confidence, eh, Monsieur?" Said Simeon, good liumouredly. " Exactly my term.," rejoined the nior. chant grimly. "To begin then this necklace wail, till eighteen months ago, the property of Tref. ford Khan." Simeon stoke in measured tones and observed that the solitary eye of the vendor of precious stones flashed and fin- ally closed. . . .</p>
        <p>It is a curious coincidence that the lady of this portrait wears a facsimile of the neck- lace. The large diamond in the centre proves that there is no mistake."</p>
        <p>Simeon placed the miniature and the neck- lace under the eye of Monsieur l'arlotte, who, through a magnifying &amp;atm closely examined the face painted on the ivory. His expression, however, was non-committal. Then that keen eye daneed and sparkled with an alert twinkle, which fled almost immediately. Evi- dently the 'tante of the Persian millionaire wan well known to him and the detective be. lieved that he had at him, obtained a clue to the mystery surrounding the birth of Law- son.</p>
        <p>"Who purchased the necklace from the , Khan?" questioned the jeweller, in the came hushed voice in which he had spoken after drawing the red blind over the top half of the glaze door. ' "it was stolen, and came in an indirect way into the poacession of Mr. Michael Law-</p>
        <p>r-'-fhat Monsieur Parlotte did not recognise the name was quite evident to the detective, and thus his hopes began to dwindle. Ile would attempt another venture. During the discussion the conversation had been carried lon in German, for they were cautious melt; but now Simeon apoke in his native tongue, , much to the surprise of his vis.a-,is, nine was I preparing a tour de force. Beneath the master eyes of the detective, the jeweller' single orb flickered. "Monsieur Parlotte," said Simeon in hie gravest manner, "you can assist me in clear. lug up the mystery surrounding Univ lady of the miniature and the diamond necklace if you will. At the present moment an impor. tent link is missing. Let me have a eine, only the smallest to go upon, then leave the working to those whose business it is to uas. ravel the plot. Am I to appeal ill vain!" Parlotte u eye never quitted the portrait. lie paid no heed to the detective, being iust in thought. . . . .</p>
        <p>" I al positive that in coming to you for help I have sought the right man! Don't refuse me." Parlotte looked troubled.</p>
        <p>"You will thank me hereafter," Simeon went on. "If I cannot apeak more plainly, pardon the prudence, but reserve is impera- tive. Monsieur Parlotte, a little clue, anti soon the finish."</p>
        <p>sad rtate of affairs," said the melt, chant. "1 must think it over."</p>
        <p>"Still reluctant/ I beg you not to waste precious hours in unreasonable caution." "I have no desire, Monsieur, to offend you or to waste your time or my own. I must obey my conscience." "You drive me to despair,' said Simeon, biting his lip. " Why rake over the ashes of dead memo- ries?'' asked l'arlotte, almost peevishly. "Becanse the dead are at peace, their race is run, It is the living for whom I am fight- ing," said Simeon, with • show of sentiment which astounded his companion. "'t here are at stake the birthright of a man, the happiness of a lovely and amiable girl, who is held in bondage by a woman who is defrauding and separating two young people for • purpose of her ou n. During my residence in England I have mixed with gond comrades whom I would gladly serve." Ills chin stink on his breast, his hands clasped behind his back, and he paced the confined space in deep thought. Then he spoke again. "Some twenty years or so ago, 1 was ordered to Russia." Ile paused, for Parlotte seemed strangely preoccupied and uneasy. "1 travelled to Moscow, to Peter.- burgh. A reign of terror nos !hen setting in, the moo jika were thirsting to revenge the tyranny of the autocracy. The ambles were eying from their country seats, the capital , was crowded, and the fair young wife of—" , "Hush! hush!" cried Parlotte, all of • ' tremble "Mon Dieu mon Dieu!" ha es. claimed, ' wringing his hands. "Not here—. not here—do not murmur that name."</p>
        <p>"Why this fear?" demanded Simeon bend- ing low, the better to catch Parlotte's wins. per. "Russian spies are everywhere. I suspect the very walls. My house is watched, my servants are bribed, my lifc is a misery, and I know not what to do. But " And here the merchant dress himself up. self-respect- ing dignity on every feature. '• I will never betray my client's business—their confidence I. sacred. What say sou, Monsieur Simeon,"</p>
        <p>IDANTSI VOWS I. Ire • Me. bat IIiSTTLOTS RATS &amp; MICE et•Ti.:= DANYSZ VIRUS, Ltd" Boa I.4•Amtoall bt Gledel, le. Mai</p>
        <p>"That you are quite riffs. "Should I disappear--" "Disappear!" Who knows? I am a suspect, in league with the revolutionist party now in Paris. Wrongly, it is true, I am supposed to be the Almoner of those supplying the funds for ammunition and weapons which are now be- ing smuggled into Russia." '•ls there any foundation for the sus- picion?" " None, beyond the fact that I Ware ad- vanced money on valuables." " We will be answerable for your safety," said the detective reassuringly.‘ "It can hardly be said that I am asking you to be- tray confidence. I want some particulars of these, that is all," and, as he spoke. he gathered the necklace and the miniature from the table and began to pack them in the cardboard box again. "I take it this is an incident in your pro- fession?" asked Parlottc.</p>
        <p>" Exactly so." " I never make a deal on supposition." 'Nor I," agreed Simeon. " Our interests are mutual?"</p>
        <p>..... " Never lose sight of the fact that I am in the black books of the Russian police. 'those ledgers you see on the shelves are merely bogus books purposely misleading." Simeon smiled appi oval. " Every page has been searched, at night my office has been invaded, bet the spice en- terprising visits have been without result." Parlotte smiled. and Simeon catching his hand pulled him close. " Where are the real ledgers" .. • •,.</p>
        <p>No, no, Monsieur, not even to you will I tell that much. If von will submit to he blindfolded I might be. induced to give you such information as I possem." Is there no alternative?" " None, and you most promise not to re- move the bandage until I give you permis- sion?"</p>
        <p>" I willingly promise that, Monsieur Par.</p>
        <p>--;t-Pncoiaditionally?" " Most certainly." Parlotto took a silk muffler from a drawer and tied it over the detective's eyes. " Do not move. Monsieur," he said, warn- ingly, a. he wheeled the interior fittings of • Shannon roll desk from the miter casing. dinclosing a square hole and a short wooden ladder. " Monnieur, we are about to descend a ladder, feel your way ntep by step," mid Parlotte, guiding the detective'. feet. When on the last rung he pulled a cord, and the desk rumbled into its former position. The rush of air through the winding passage was as the coldness of the grave, and Simeon shivered an his outstretched hand encoun- tered the clammy stone wall'a. He heard the pening and closing of a door and knew that their journey hati..erme to an end. . .</p>
        <p>not gtir till the lamp is lighted," said Parlotte adjusting the reflector no that it only illuminated a portion of the crypt, the remains of a church buried tinder a compara- tively modern improvement of l'aris. Par- lotto loosened tho bandage which had FO effectively shut out Simmit's vision, and the dassling glare of light made the detective for a second or two unable to distinguish into what kind of place he had been brought. " Here, Monsieur," said Parlotte, lifting • bulky brass-bound volume from a shelf and depomiting it on a reading desk, which had in other days belonged to some old monas- tery, " Here in this page is the information you oak for." " You grudge reading the story," said Simeon, looking at the roughly chiselled stones and chafing the backs of his cold hands.</p>
        <p>"Small profit," said ParTolle sagely. "I'll guarantee you a big return," re- plied the detective. "Those words are your bond?" "Assuredly." "You are a man of honour, I know." Par- lotte bowed low; after making a memo- randum of the conversation in a pocket diary he pamed it to the detective to initial. "You are a great man, Monsieur Simeon."___ . . .</p>
        <p>'Tuisti, minutes are flying fast. We need not indulge either in compliments or triviali- ties:: _</p>
        <p>"God : then we will to businege."</p>
        <p>" And let o be brief," said Simeon. Parlotte gave a dry business-like eongo, placed his finger on an entry in the ledger and read an extract. " Diamond necklace val. £16,000 English sterling security for • loan of 100,000 franc,"</p>
        <p>.The date?" asked Simeon. 1881." "Who was the borrower?" "A Russian Grand Duke." , "Name." . _</p>
        <p>"I can-not disclose it. "You evidently object to mentlontng names," said Simeon, shrugging his shoul- ders. "Mere business caution, no doubt." "I have learned to be wary," said Partette. "Very well. Continue, if you please." "Interest paid for twelve months. Re- deemed January, 1862, previous to the mar- riage of the aforesaid Grand Duke." Simeon's stern face became sterner. and Parlotte, anticipating the cause, said quietly. "This is fresh news to you, and has eluded your department, but even the secret Bureau is not infallible, and Parlotto et CM are as elm, as the tomb."</p>
        <p>"Do on," rejoined the detective curtly. "The Grand Duke's union was marred by his open infidelities, and his outraged wife eloped in company of • young English gentle- man Inking in tier flight from Petersburg that, diamond necklace. The husband en• deavoured to compel her to surrender it, as it was an heirloom, but the Grand Duchess demurred, ne she considered it to be her own to do with as she chose."</p>
        <p>"Adultery," said Simeon frigidly. "Justifiable in her eaae, Monsieur," said Parlone.</p>
        <p>"Humph!" grunted the detective, who was a _great stivkler for the rights of the husband. "Whatever were the wrongs of a wife, she should never ignore the moral code of so- ciety." "Such sentiment, would have suited her husband. I may say, in parenthesis, that the lady's escapade was ignored by the Czar and, of rourbe, the noble dames of the day fol- lowed suit. Six months after the 'reparation, the Grand Duke expired suddenly at the dinner table."</p>
        <p>_ ... "And the cause?" asked Simeon, now tho- roughly interested. Parlotteh solitary eye ram!. cloned. and dosed again. There was a tendency to seal his lips, but he got the better of his habitual caution.</p>
        <p>"Poison in his wine, it was said," be whis- pered in tones of awe. "Ever traced?" "The Russian police are blunderers. Seve- ral were suspected. but nothing certain ever came to light. Ah, Monsieur Simeon, they require men of your intelligence in the Mus- covite branch of the Secret Service." "You Ratter me." "No, no. A simple opinion expressed by an admirer of your abilities." "Tut, tut." Simeon was secretly pleased, though he endeavoured to conceal the fact. "And the fate of the Grand Duchess?'</p>
        <p>"She was young, handsome, free; she had the world at her feet, and she married her lover."</p>
        <p>- handed, of course," muttered Simeon abstractedly. "A daughter was born to her in Peters- burgh. The past had sunk into oblivion, and She parents were domiciled in Russia. MO baby girl grew to wordanhood, charming of face, figure, and character. She was con• sidered by those who met her to be the belle of the Csar's Court. She wedded Prince— you mentioned the name, therefore no Deed for me to say it." Simeon placed his hands under his coat- tails and considered the uses to which he could put the meagre information supplied by the one-eyed merchant. He was an expe- rimenter, his thoughts were racing in a new direction, and he began to see his way to the proper development of the clues already in his hand.</p>
        <p>"Parlotte, coo out - Herod Herod in your caution." said he; a grim smile playing about Os lips. "Von dread speaking n•mes even in this isolated cell."</p>
        <p>I have calla, Russian spies are like moles, ever burrowing in the dark. Use lies become second nature. I wish to die in my bed, Monsieur, and to do that I must mt.. rise caution everywhere. The Prince of whom I speak was loyal to the Czar and his country. He was incorruptible, a heinous sin where all are tubber,. An honest man in • nest of</p>
        <p>thieves is a nein/tic,. At the period of which I am giving you particulars, there was an embroilment between England, Russia, and Persia. An important document from the Shah to the Czar wan missing. The up- right Prince, so his enemies insinuated, was the traitor. Whether he was or was not none could say, hut he disappeared, some say, de- ported to Siberia; others that he was im- plored in • fortress. Nothing official has been divulged." "Ile may be living still," suggested Simeon's hard, strong voice. "Possibly. That is within your province to prove." "And the fate of his wife, what of that?"</p>
        <p>" She was secretly warned and fled. it is be- lieved, to Persia sided by—" Parlotto ithecked himself. . .</p>
        <p>"By whom?" said Simeon, his hand tightoning on the speaker's shoulder. Par- lotte did not directly answer. " Not an elopement this time, but the re• mit of a political necessity. She was smuggled )ut of Russia by the man who bonght the document."</p>
        <p>" And thin lady of whom yin are 'peaking took the necklace in her flight." " The question is, did she use it to bribe that man? For years every capital in Europe has been ransacked, but some wit, whose in:, rest it was to delude. him put the police on the wrong scent. Even you were homlwinked into believing that I had the Light of Ilea's in my safe. Who was your informal?" Ilia one eye twinkling inquisitively. "Parlotte, like you, 1 never mention names. Yon have told MP all? It amounts to very little." Simeon's disappointment was k, en, and Parlotte's one eye wore a teasing expression. " Inadequate'?" said Simeon mournfully. "Certainly not conclusive."</p>
        <p>Simeon, while apenking, took in the jewel. lees method, concluding that there ens more Information yet to come and he wan right. it in the princess that you wish to trace?" "I do." "She in alive." "Where?" "I do not know." "Then I am as far off 11% ever."</p>
        <p>" Monsieur Simeon should not jump hastily to conclusions. A lady of my acquaintance indulges in cards and roulette," . . . . .</p>
        <p>" Nuothing to do with My Case," exclaimed Sinumn, losing him temper..,</p>
        <p>Wrong, very wrong. Do you know the abode of the Leopardess?" Parlotte's eye opened and shut excitedly, he was taking rise out of the detective and he could not re- train his antigfaction.</p>
        <p>" No, I see you do not. Bear in mind lam not speaking to Rafael Simeon, of the Bureau de Police, but to Monsieur Rafael Simard, the gentleman, the man of honour who will not directly or indirectly use the in formation Recorded him, to the detriment of the lady of her small and exclusive Monte Carlo." _ _ .</p>
        <p>Simeon spread hie open hands on his hr,•nst, bowing solemnly. " The Leopardess," said Parini le, "is a Indy formerly in the pay of Germany. Rot having committed an error, she prefers to In ell in Paris, rather than aubmit to thro tender mercy of the Chancellor, who is minded to place her safely under leek and</p>
        <p>"Afraid of her tongue?" "She serves France now." Parlotte looked at Simeon'. astonished c,-untenance in his moat mocking manner. "Having retired from the fatigues of active polities, she gambles and Ilan for her guests a select few, who never eimak of the aalon they frequent nightly. fler salon in charm- ing. Come (here with me, come to-night. I will introduce you, and she will warmly wel- come the distingtibbed Rafael Simeon." "I have three friends staying at the IliStel RI. James. May they be of the party?" "What are their names?" "Captain Archer Chinnery, the Honourable Alec Blandford, and—" here the detective made an impressive pause to mark the effect " Mr., Miehael Lawson."</p>
        <p>Parlotte moved not a muscle, nor did his expressive orb close, a habit when he was at all disturbed. Evidently the name conveyed nothing to him. " When the belle are clicking merrily and greedy frenzy at its height, you may catch the Leopardess gliding from table to table. Then you can waylay her and beg two minutes pri vale conversation. Devote one minute in flattery, praise her eyes, her hair, her com- plexion—it is very artificial, nevertheless, praise it to the sky. The second minute dangle the miniature before Madame's eyes. She will tell you—perhaps—where the origi- nal is to be found."</p>
        <p>Simeon's listening attitilde changed to one of sharp surprise. Ile VC 110 completely taken unawares. But with elaborate carelessness he buttoned his coat across his chest, polished his hat, and politely requested Monsieur Par. little to again blindfold him and to lead him back to the office whence they had come.</p>
        <p>(To b. Continued).</p>
