        <p>! Lodgings for Working Class Womon. —"I he housing problem for houtelew single comet], is one which several sere iphilanthropic institutions attempt to dad, and an effort is to be made to link there up into • National Association for carrying on this work. There Is to hen meeting on the subject at the Guildhall in London, next month, attended by the Lord Mayor, and at which the Duchess of Marlborough will occupy the chair. 'the close of women and girle who have to feud for themselves from their earliest youth, are better able to make their way in life, than those who are brought up In comfortable homes and then suddenly left without provision, but it is for the former that efforts are mostly being made, to provide decent accommodation at prices which they can afford to pay. I. this, Scotland has led the way, but Manchester hts followed the example of Glasgow in building and furnishing the magnificent Aehton Douse, the first municipal lodging-house for women in England. Emigration Facilitim.—Emigration has lost many of the drawbacks that etteeded it ellen the magnetic west first began to attract settlers from the old country and there are being formed in many Canadian towel leagues to assist tho established inuniarant to briog Oct his wife and family from honie. Organised efforts are also being made to creel° in Canada replicas of the towns at home, no that those going from the one to the other, will find the some indostriaband the same Clans of people they base been sccostomed to at borne. There is also now-a.daye, the strictest sopervision over the various &amp;geode. mimed in satisfying the clamorous demand from Canada for women home-maker., sod domeetic servants. lo the early days of America the Virginia Company used to offer to girls willing to go oat, a free outfit sod peasage, but they were practically sold on arrival. The highest bidder had to marry his macho.° forthwith and one of these wife-sales is des- cribed by Miss Mary Johnston in her romance "By Order or the Company." e. . . .</p>
        <p>Letter Writing.—One often hears that the art of letter writing is dead, and if that Is no, it is the post card, and the daily Press combined, that have killed it. Formerly people ussd to write to one another about the public news of the day, but all such information in now the common property of all, and to a great extent it leaves the skilled letter-writer with "nothing to say " beyond what will go into a very small corn. pass. But women, it is generally admitted, write better lettern than men, and according ton French authority this is because woman has boat none of her enigmatical qualities in her struggles for equality. There in, be says, •Iwa3s something more than the obvious meaning to look for in • wornan's letter" a veiled mewling" which he suggests given it • merit of Its own. The Masculine View of Spring Cleaning.—Natare lover. are rejoicing at the many algae of advancing Sprlog, bet there Is one of them—au sure a• the return of the migratory birds, which is eeeee very welcome, and that I. the preparations for the Sprier! cleaning. Meo will raver understand that this is neceseary, or that any of the dirt and work In of their making. When I it I. pointed out that It must be done they assume the injured air of people with a grievance, and go and grumble and condole with their male friends In similar ' plight. The comic artiat stretches hi, imagination to the utmost in picturing the ordeal. he has to go through. "Why cannot one room be done •t • time," says the" mere man," hut It is not airway. posaible to have in the sweep on miswrite occasions, for each room, or to make aeparate jobs of the t king up of carpets, and various processes incidental to Spring cleaning. , Of course the capable manager will get through it all , with much iOSS confusion than the incapable, but in any lase it would help matter. if men were more patient sad logical on such necessities, Instead of, a. iu usually the case, adding to the general diecomfort by Igrombling, fault-finding, and making the worst of things. The Care of Corsets.—Corsets are expensive items o • wornan's wardrobe, hut the live, of these may b oonsiderably prolonged by curd f ul attention, an 1m eliding. &amp;fore searing, it is wise to remove th ribbon usually used for binding the top and to replan It with white or coloured webbing, so the case may be</p>
        <p>similar to that used for the waint of a skirt. The same may be done at the bottom of the corset where the bones often protrude noon after being worn. When the steels end boors been worn through the material, little strip. of old white kid gloves shoidd be sewn neatly over the itart, and should the msterial le eplit lengthwise, linen tape, the net:emery width, no be ntsehined wetly on huh aide., but both cotton and stitching nowt he strong. IF webbing be auhatituted for binding, before the stays are so. n it will he found that much lime and trouble will he raved. Waists and Collars.—There will be a tendency later 0. to lower the waist line, and the Empire line will .0t le exclasively worn. The nerii ponctied corsage no belt, girdle howely buttoned with knot. at one site taking the place of the letter. Very chic or. the black tailored drew. tr:nunett with horn buttons sad taffetas bow—quite lerge---et the throat, tied loosely just under the chin. Navy serge lies now lost it. popularity, howover, end dark blue ratin end aerte-desoie are well to the somo models is the latter materiall are made to open at the side. Over , planets of blue and white Pekin, end the Mee Swath pin- ' striped with white. Many of the coats hare sailer coll.n coming down in • 111.11 point in front, and • few Instead of being eailor eloped are sloped down Into • point belied, linishins with a tassel at the waist. The Ready-Made Little IFrocka."—Woederful are the.. little frock.' our beat drapery brumes are turn- ing out, in soch charming variety, too, that one may waken selection e ithout fear cf meeting another purchaser arrayed in mostly nrnilnr drew. 'This enterprise, on the pert of the big houses, renders its quite independent of the.' little dressmaker," who has played an importoot part in natty a woman's dress acharmes,and *hoes work was not alwao. satisfactory— for she ha. often failed to green her patroeess'a mean- ing as to tern out a hook worthy of Panqula or Worth. For the yomag girl these dresres are ideefaed really mean • connidenable raving in es,ienre, not, to mention worry and patience. Neely Victorian Note—Flower. are naturally suited to the young giil, and certaihly thin year there inn wealth of Nature'. Liken blooms no cleverly and beautifully imitated th it it in difficult to discern the artificial from the natural. Rose, for the young girl are avenged in flat poetic., remielecent of early Victorian dam indeed in every detail of the young def ottani. vrardrole, one in struck I hie season with thin toot*. Wraps to cover the dminty frock only heighten thecharm of the latter by artordine a moat effective hungyound. Some of these are military, other..,. Empire In ?amigo. Roth ere made of the most nipple cloth, sod trimmed with contrasting material.. V rep. for garden potties are of dullest richest patina mod the silkiest of eachmire, and the linings •re °flee of changing hue.. gocentrio Headgetr.—iome of tlae newest hat• ars eccentric to a r'estee, notably thoee with the large crown.. crumpled like a concertina. Theme have very little trimming, an ornament of oolonred hemp sufficing. Fruit is very largely used as decorations, hot often the resemblance to tiny known vatiety is Might. Plume and noodle., which scarcely lend themselves to millinery purposes are often 0.11, on many bizarre shapes. Toque. and torbsne get every day higher In the crow., toct one feature of the looting millinery is the popularity of the clone-tiling hat,</p>
