        <p>The Chairm‘n ntiS Ponketh had said they were trilling to divert all their own sewage and ao, he supposed, relieve themselves of the obligation to con- tribute any money torverde the echeme. It. believed, , that it ass a physical impossibility for them to divert the whole of the sewage that they now poured into the brook. Mr. Sherratt : Seeing that they have already contr. bated to the couditi rn of tho hr oak. whet th•y may intend to do in the future has not leg to do with the question of severing. The Chairman said he believed that if Penketh agreed to divert their sewage they voold be free from liability to contribute to the Ror•I District (7ouncire rcheme. howevyr, maiwained that it ...old no mpoteitile for them to do that. It would c not them mum than ha!f their •hare in the scheme.</p>
        <p>Mr. Farness if you eerie, to pollute. your liability in gone. As long we you pollute, you •re liable. Mr. Balton considered the estimated cost of £152 woe encessive. Tender, ought to be advertised for. The Clerk anid tbat matter wen in the hands of the Rural District Council. The Palish Council cniild only suggest. •Ithough it bid to hod the tuoney or half of it. Mr. Halton said it would probobly be well to con- sider the advisability of carrying Ott whom, +bleb ROUld serve the ',enable for mnny years. They would no doubt be able to obtain a loan front the Local Government Board, to be replaced in 25 or CO years, and the yolinges gene•mLon would thou have to bear their share of the outlay. The Clerk pointed out that before a loan could be obliCned n Local U /vernment Board inspector would have to hold a public hooters, Mr. Halton proprwei aol Mr. Sterrett seconiod the following reiolu ion •—" l'hot thin r Atom! desire, to point out that the brook is a boon/wry book dividing the psrinhes of Great Sinkey and Ponketb and the claim is made that. inasmuch as cartnin sewers Croon Pooket , discharge into the broo.o, that township should be called upon to contribute to the cost of any scheme which may be necessary to abate or remove the ntlissace com- 'deiced of." . . .</p>
