        <p>The Coronation Arrangements. Sir,--Nothing much in the eay of detail as to the arrangementa for the celebration ef the Coronation Day in this town has yet beccine public. It seems, however, to be certain that the day, Thuraday. June 22nd, is to be celebrated by a procession of the 'Friendly Societies in the morning, sad a gala for the r e school children in Victoria Park in the afternoon. These two frets mem to me to warrant serious miegiv loge as to the possibility of carer ing out the astral Walking Day pr, gramme on the following day without considerable hardship and with any degree of success. I need hardly enumerate the considerations which give rim to such misgivings. To those inti- mately experienced in organisirg and preparing they will be °helot]. ; by others they will perhaps be scarcely appreciable. The childten will be tired and •• a worn i tit, Many Sunday School Teachers and oche:. who do the work cn Walking Day will be arduously engaged practically all Thursday and will also be tired and fatigued. It will be impossible to make the necessary preparations on Thursday and next to impossible to assemble the children and other people concerned in tine for the precession on Friday morning. For these and other reasons I fear the procession if it tr oh place would be but Hat, stale and unprofitable. I suggest therefore that it would be wise, for this occasion, to emit all ogrther the usual Sunday school procession on Friday morning, A ". but to pr ceed with the customary treat .., i este the childrrn in the afternoon as usual. It Is . ats„, l. elprohld be at ily to depute the children of this. ant° , r,, ella•kiNilo no would leave them no better off this Ciororrarion , a year than in other year.. Some of course will not be r, pleaseel. with this auggestion, but then it is the Corozotoon year and we cannot all expect to be pleased. ..' - I sin prepared to promote end carry out the sugges- . lion I have made an far as I can. NEVILLE WILSON, .11 41. yary's ' . '</p>
        <p>,4</p>
        <p>The Rector and Rome. Sir. am not surprised at your tlesire to clogs thin cortespoodetire Ind it has certainly done good ant evidenced by the remarks it your correspondent " Straightforward." I agree with Lim in condemn- , ing the Itomeward movement of the Rector and his •' • "" adherents at the Parish church and think that if they belie Se what they teach and practice they ought st leaetto len,re the Church of England. While he draws salary from the Anglican Church he ought to con- form to its dogmas and doctrines. I must agree sith the Rector that in strict legal parlance the Church of Rome is only on sufferance in England and that is perhais the reason why lie does not follow the example set by his great predecessors Newman and Manning ad join it. Turning to your Papistical cot respondents I. with them, regret that this correspondence has not risen to a higher level, but, for that, the blame does nit rest upon me. I have traversed and cm.troverted most of their di game of belief and methods t.f government and they have kept discreetly silent, running after another person whom I suppose they c. alit mote easily combat. Let me remind these gentlemen that in referring t Henry VIII. they rood. ton one of the leading pillars of their own church ; for did not the Pope give him the title of " Defooder of the Faith " ? The insults levelled at Elintbeth and Cromwell are equally tut of place for these though far from perfect were above the average of their Papal contemporaries in respecta- bility and were true pstriotic LAN rs of their country. Corning t, the present daj, and reviewing the i,religion amongst us I sok what is the Church of Rome doing to advance religi. us thought and encourage reverence for the Sabbath Day ? Hun not the Pope condemned modern th,ught in a recent encyclical and hos he not lut a restraint en the freedom of marriage by refurbishing one of the musty , old decrees of the past ? What doee she teach as al Church in her young men's societies. I gave in one ' of my letters a description of nu item Spain under the Papacy atoll Fee in 3 oar last Mane in the Church news you give a repot tof a lecture on Romanistu in Belgium. Let llle commend these to your Papist readers for their edification and reproof and sign myself. —Yours &amp;e.,</p>
        <p>PHIL SQUOIL</p>
        <p>,401 V tie Icrs \l.._. to the i Wk # ' EDITOR.. _....-------,:tai.</p>
