      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Council and the Suffrage.</head>
        <p>The Council and the Suffrage.</p>
        <p>Si,.— Points of iew are funny things Aly letter is Women's Suffrage Las evidently caused bOl7llO . _</p>
        <p>Let as see wherein lies the offence. I said ,—" At the fag end of a more thou th en hours Hitting—when they (the coma ihots) were in n conditien to pass anything, they passed on resolution—the terna;ning membet s did—east mmmci. eery grate st oil to them" Comm it he that the Suffragists take exception to the phrase "alien they were in a condition to pies anything?" Well previous to the resolution they had rustled the Finance Bill through si ith scarcely word of comment a aired comprehensive, import- ant and well-prepared budget, and one that merited careful considetation—and tl.e natural inference to me was that tiny were tired out. Nothing more than that was implied and nothing less. . . . .</p>
        <p>In reply to ore of htr et itice (J.P.J.) "More in Sorrow than in Argot" would be still more "in reuse' if by using s now de plume she allows',) A most unfair suspicion to rest is the others of being the writer (or at ntv rite of being in agreemeid with her) in her "" indiscriminating criticism." She also greatly deprecates the use of the word " favour " in regard to the passing of the resolution. There is s great deal to much of this conciliatory attitude from women to men on this snhject. I thought we were working for principle and justice, but if the Conocillois •ho passed the resolution did it as a " favour:: to my va.ind it loses its value.</p>
        <p>May Imy to the officials of the Suffrage Society, that if being a member of their Society denies one the right of free epeach iu regard to a public meeting open to public mticiam, theft that Society in weakening its own efforts. lam well aware of the trouhle taken by the Society in getting the Bitt circulari,ed ar.d " thoroughly appreciate " it, and am alai) aware of the cLurteel of its officials no less than lam f the courtesy of thoto Councillors who were courtoona on the Ith inet.</p>
        <p>Ibis is Ins final word on the subject.- Yours truly. MORE IN SORROW THAN IN ANGER.</p>
        <p>Sir,--In your issue dated the 15th instant, there appears such ail extraordinary, though nevertheless for certain tit aeons interesting, letter signed by the princir a's if the Wars ingt a Woalto'a Sull rage Society that I h rise ap, logy is flquired in requesting year permission to accept the following observation. thereon . _</p>
        <p>It appears that the Warrington Town Council recently at the end of • tire hours' sitting paased a resolution in favour of petit'oning His Majesty's Government to giant early facilities for extending the Parliamentary Francine., to woolen ratep.yers. A correspondent ,ct y or columns commenting on this fact stated at the fag end of a more titan three hours sitting, . . . . they were in a condition to pass anything, .... and we are very grateful to them. The first letter to which I refer saye that the writer of the foregoing " does not knom or does not thorrughly appreciate the methods of our Sociaty." The letter proceeds tostate that every Alderman and Councillor of Warrington was circularised and" meet of the mernbes of the Town Council personally interviewed." . . .</p>
        <p>No wondir we then wad " Their minds were naturally made tip on the Suffrage question before they earns to the Town Council meriting." Th sitter statement is an amazing admission and it would perhaps he well if Warrington ratepayers would romider and " th, roughly appreciate the methods of Werringt n soffiagists. Apart from the fact that not • single member of the Council was elected by the rater ayers to repret.ent his or her opiniim we have the brazen statement that members of the Town Council after haring been personally interviewed went ti the Town had with minds "made up ' not having heard one word for the other sided the res. lotion. . . .</p>
        <p>-Contrast such conduct, which is absolutely unpar- donable in a public representative, wi h the metl,ods ent ployeAl and the t.uocesstul lf salt. obtained by those who oppose the granting of voles to women. Without ant fussy peraonal interteret cc reply postcards have</p>
        <p>CoaoutTar: Trausparaot Cement for 01TaN Cbiaa. ap, 110., .pd le.tost !TN.—Advt.</p>
        <p>been sent out to women ratepayers in various parts of the country and in 67 districts which have been canvassed the result is : Total electorate canvased Number opposed to woman suffrage 30,031 Number in fa•our of „ „ 11,571 Number not answered And it it reasonable to suppose that nearly all those who did not answer are egsinst W 012130 suffrage for, according to the methods of both Warrington and other suffragiste, if they were not they wadi act be silent, unless Ilamb—Yours Ac-, JOHN W. G. BEAUMONT. Manchester, April 20th, 1911.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Rector and La Milo.</head>
        <p>The Rector and La Milo.</p>
        <p>Sir,—'• We court criticism and only fear prejudice." Such is the heading which forms part (4 the other- tiaemente of the exhibitions taking place nightly this week at the Hippodrome. I take up the clmllerige. I haven' en the pietare en the posters at the corners of the streets and the scene, on the stage have been described to me. I are told that crowds of young people of both sexes have attended the entertainments.</p>
        <p>I desire to flak the authorities what good end is served by them, exhibitions of living statuary ? Is it the advancement of art? Is it the encouragement of , innocent love of the beautiful? Is it the increase of I purity and motality ? I fear not. Indeed I am certain that the object is simply the making of money and the result a distinct loss of delicacy au refinement, if no worse. A heavy responsibi.ity rests upon those who provide end th. se who allow these entertainments in our tonnAnd in the iaereate of the young I earnestly protest Nothing would have induced me to t.ke part in the opening ceremoniis of the Hippodrome if I had known that it was intended to open it 0..1 Sundays fur gain, and that it was going to be used on weekdays for what I consider questionable exhibitions. I am. Sir, yours truly FREDERIC W. WILLIS. The Rectory, Warrington, April 21/t, 1911.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>WELL-KNOWN HORSE-DEALERS CLAIM. – A Case Six Years Old.</head>
        <p>WELL-KNOWN HORSE-DEALERS CLAIM. - A Case Six Years Old.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thurscley before his Honour Judge 11 Y Stanger li.C. Mr. Harold Smith M.P., (instructed by klessra. Hobert Davie. and Co.) appeared for Mr. William Enoch Stnikey of Croft, who sued. Messrs. Walker and Hontfrays, Brevrere, Salford, for Vitt in payment of • bay mare, which he alleged he had sold to them in March 1965. The defendants were represented by Mr. Wyngate Saul (ion rutted by Messrs. Sutton and Co., Mancheater ) Mr. Smith in opening the case said that the plaintiff wee a horse dealer and in March 19415 the defendants' reptesentative—either • Mr. Allen or ft Mr. Morgan— called on the plaint ill and purchased two homes, one a bey roare (the ott ject of the sction)md theother a roan gelding. The pinion.e price. paid for the animals were LOH and £57 rospectively.The point at issue between the parties was as to the pnrtictilar contract and more particularly RS to the conditiobs under which the lmrse wits aold. Ilia client said that the horses had to be pas ed by • veterinary surgeon and hod to ie subjected to two days' trial tome it hells., they were good ocr ken I root. Both boxes were sent to the defendant. on the 3lst March aod arrived on the mime day. A iLay or ta o later a defendants' representatit e tetephoned to the plaintiff and told him that the bay mare auited, but they were returning the roan gelding. The latter was returned and aloog e Bit it another horse, the defendants milting the plaintiff to find a home for it. The other hone Cu, foiled to he some- what of a ',rock" but shown was pi...cured. On April 25tb the defendants wrote that the mare had puemonia and that they lied worked it for two days before its illness developed. The plaintiff immediately repudiated responaibility and imitated out that the horse beitmged to defendants as the time of trial MIS over. The IBSSOD that the action had been delayed so lung was femme the ploininli wasted to arrive at a settlement without going into Court, as be had never toed soyone before. The question foe his Honour to deoid.wno..hrn the property in the horse pasted and he (Mr. Smith) asked him to say that the property passed on the expiration of two dais trial. No businms mm, be mid, would e'er dream of making a contract of a month's trial of a horse ris the defendants contendad. If the defendants did not consider that the mare was theirs when it died why was it that they dint not send to the plaintiff the proceeds of the sale on the carcase ? The plaintiff in maser to Mr. Smith denied having received the following letter from the defendants davit April lot, 1900.—" We have received the two horses on one month trisl. We are not lobe resporaibla for ailment, accident or death, nhil.t in our charge ; so charge to he made for the version of the horst...during the month." If be hnd received that letter he would have asked for the horses to he returned as Ire would have considered such &amp;contract unreasonable. Ile also denied that there wm any el ratagemontt on his ;art that the animal should Inc returned alter it became ill, nor did he promise to go mil see it. Ile was not acciattomed to make coon:oils whereby he insured a home tots month after leaving his potamiion. The defendant. dia tor, .a for lobe could recollect, inform him of the mare's death or of how they had cispoeed of the carcase. . . . .</p>
        <p>In ellllNer to Mr. Saul, plaintid ssid there might have been one no lit fay occasion en which Le sold horses to the Manchester Corporation non month'. trial, but he did not think no. Ile denied the receipt of a letter front the defendant company in which it was stated that the terms of purchase were to be a mt nth's trial.</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith said the letter advanced euch outrageoue ternm Mat, in the absence of prompt repudiation of them by tie plintitl, it WOD UtittlinOsbi• Lind it, could have been received. Hie Honour declined to admit evidence as to the custom prevailing in tbe bade. . . .</p>
        <p>For the defendant company, Henry It. Allen, manager (the stable departmen•, spoke to making the purchase from Mr. Sankey. Ile said the acreement was that the horses should to sent on a months trial at platntiff s risk, Mr. Sank y stating that those were the terma on which he supplied the horses to the Alancheater Con. porstion. The animals actually sent were not those which witm xi selected, and one was subsequently returned. In communications about the animal w hicb died, Mt. Sankey made no suggestion as to the two days' trial having been exceeded. Witnesa telephoned plaintiff informing him of the death of the mare. Richard Robinson, who wax also present w hen the contract was made, corroborated. Evidence erne gives of the eriting end dispatch of letter to the plaintiff, naming the cond I ion of a month's tt _ . .</p>
        <p>Henry CLarles Harrison, veterinary surgeon to the defendset company, stated that soon after the purcheae lie passed tbe mare as sound. Soon afterwards it con- tracted pneumonia and died. He repeatedly telephoned 'to the plaintiff as to the condition of the mare en I Mr Sankey said nothing an to the mare not being his property, but confined his remarks t r the question of treatment. Crider ordinary circumstances the items of contract wt ich the defendants alleged plaintiff had entered Into would rather surprise witness, but an exception might be mace in a first transaction with the object of procuring new business. Mr. Wingate Saul contended that if Mr. Sankey bad had any crrnfidence in his one he would have brought ' the action immediately, instead of allowing six years to slew. It would be dangermrs t a not on Mr. Sankey, recollection of what took plane %hen theta , was such a great deal of evidence to contradict him. Mr. Smith submitted that it would have by en impossible for Mr. Sankey to forget the receipt of letter which contained such outrageous propteele Plaintiff's frankness in the box wee at times a little alarming ;he gave his answers entirely considering the truth and not king else. The terms on which elmost every horse denier made cobtracts were two days' trial. It wee ridiculous to suppose that Mr. Sankey would hand over the mere to e firm of brewers to work it as they liked, to leave it mending bot or cold at street corners, and perhaps to feel it imaitisfactorily, and yet for the risk to be entirely on his side. Such condi- tions would make his business impossible. Ilia Honour Raid that the action raised Immo import- ant points end he would defer his judg uent for a fort- night. He supposed it would be impossible for the per tee to arrive at u friendly settlement ?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
