        <p>DR. LAW MR. H. M. ASTI roN as " Ilacteth " an Nampo and and MR. H. G. 3fOSi.EY MRS. R. f: ARNF.IT as King Duncan. as Lady Macbeth. We would remind rur readers that the tuuch-dierusie I performance of " ilicbeth " I y local amateurs will take place at the Royal Court Theatre on Monday I caning Lent. The proceeds will he denoted to the Infirmary Million Penny Fund and as the whole of the rsaerved poi tion of the theatre has been baled op and there is a steady demand for ticket, for the p polar pare, a crow led Immo is a foregone conclusion. Dr. Law and his company have been bard at work on .he c acludiog relit arsals this week and a finished performance of Shakespeare's !peat tragedy is ant,cipated. REFLECTIONS ON " MACBETH."</p>
        <p>possess Sfacbetlfs mind and make the advaneen.ent which lie has market! out for himself (and the maims of attaining which he has imparted to hie wife—no doubt, relying upon her firm nerve to strengthen anti support his own weak nature) appear to him quite within the prospect of belief," in spite of his rerudiation. He is hurried on with daring inmetiet.ce to verify the predictions of the Sisters, and to tear abide She aril which hides the future. But he is not equal to the st uggle u,th fate awl conscience. lit' at first' bends rip each co-poral instrument to the terrible feat,- and then, when he hes ens :el upon the eommissi n of it, Lis heart n.i•gives him and he is cowed and abashed by his success. The titer!, no less dim the attempt, craft uuda As in " Hamlet the !finite is retched at the • close of the play fe ne, in the third Act ;so in "Ifsebeth " the real cimax a the Way comes in the banquet scene with tire appearance ot the Ghost ot lianrpio. ilr t r that time everything having fl ne well with Macbeth and from thence ev, rything cobspiring to his ruin.</p>
        <p>Lady Macbeth is a woman who p. ns up her emotions, who is watchful, self-cantained, win figh's against" compuneti us vi•itings of nature:. without tatting • stir be seen, or any note of vela within to escape her lips, until her heart, too aorely charged, giveaway under the weight it is forced. S. eretly and silently, to bear. The contraq of the innate force of the • i'e's charactar with the weaker. Si of the man who cannot keep Inn troubles to himself —who gi.es all Lis em lions tongue—who, when he shrinks oath hi, mind, shr"oka with his body, also- s Ito, when be ia startled mentally. Starts horiniv. and or, instead of being an If-nsatitnined, is always pouring himself out, even at tie finger tips, is very etror g'y marked in Shalieepn area delineation. We feel, instinctively that, though Nfaclxih, under the fatal spell of the weird sisters. hail had his damnable thoughts, of the ssift way to the promised height of sovereign,y. roused aud warmed into life by their suggestions--it still needed a power of earth, in the urging force of his wife's will, to be joined to the powers of hell ;s ithout which. Duncan would have slept unharmed in Ilacbeth's castle. The reaction of disapprintinent ar:d hidden suffer- ing in Lady If .cbetir, after the crime, i acre, delicately shown. An entirely new phase in her character agents with the words, " Nought's I ad, all's apant. When our desire is pot without contrrit 'Tis safer in be that whir h we destroy, Than, by destructii.w, dwell in d übtflul joy." When Macbeth hints tuber of a new deed, n new Crime, we feel all the variety of espression which his wife's t be of "eeriness, of wonder and of dread conveyed iii t'.e question " What's to be done ? " And when he replies— '. tie innocent if the knowledge, dearest chuck. Till thou applaud the deed.' we know that she is not an are mplice ia the murder of :fa goo. _ _</p>
        <p>The subj,ct ir a deeply ii.teresting one, but (miler reflections upon it must he defernd to a lale? reca,ipn. The vrformence, trl,ich is to he giien the C. urt Thew re on Monday even ing next, trill no chubt stimulaie nanny to. more attent ve study of the s..nl sea:riling play.</p>
