      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-22_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>[By Blind Perris.]</head>
        <p>[By Blind Perris.]</p>
        <p>•• Vaulting ambition, which o'erleape itself and falls on the other."-- . . . . .</p>
        <p>Tr'rus does Shakespeare define the motive which impelled Macbeth to comph to his purpose of mounting to the throne, though, to accorni.lish it, he had to wade iu blood.</p>
        <p>" For mice own good. All reuses shall give way." And the supernatural is called in to help forward the ruin of two human souls, ready to 1.11. Woman'e nature, for which Shakespeare tumidly showe such reverence and love, he ha., in this play tineezed—has made ambition turn the mother's love to gall— the iother's love, by which in others of his characters, our souls are filled with the heart-wrung utterances of pathos and sell-forgotfulnevs. For a tote, at least, the woman here is turned to a demon. The traitor couple mur,ler their King and friend. This act they think w:11 •"I'o all (their) tights and days to come Give solely sovereign sway and rna,terdoin." They'd " jump to life to come." Yet ss Macbeth feared„ "We still have jodgment here." And so they found it. One they were no longer. Sin kept them apart. Restful nights were no longer theirs. " Macbeth shall Weep no more." " You lack the season of all nature's sleep." " AB the perfumes of Arabia will not sweeten this little hard." Days of sovereign stray they had not. no joy, no calm content. " Better be with the dead, Whom we, to gain oar peace, have ,ent to peace, Than on the torture of the mind, to lie In restless ecstasy."</p>
        <p>Judgment here, dealt, under the pangs of conscience for his wire ; death, from Macdulre sword. fir Macbeth. The les-on preached so directly by Shakesp are in this pay, that the generous are the victims of the designing and that for all crisis comes sure Nemesis to the criminal, is the invral of many of his plays, in which treachery and base ingratitude are the ruling chsracterisiice, and appears to indic.ste that at the perio I in which he was wilting, he himself was feeling the bitterness, the world-weariness, which lie expresses in his 120,h Sonnett.</p>
        <p>" For if ycu were, by my unkindness, shaken, As I, by yours, you have passed a hell of time.</p>
        <p>" Macbeth"- is the play of conscience, though the oroikings of that conscience are seen more in Lady Macbeth than in her husband. It also abates the separation from man as cell.. from God the nicer- trustless isolation, that sin brings in itediain, Itarkimas and ts rmr cm n close in upon the guilty ones. .</p>
        <p>There meat have been consultations on Dunctn's murder before the play opens, for Lady Macbeth slays What beast wasn't then, That madeyou break this enterprise to me ?' though some critics have held the pini a that to Lady Macbeth is to be att,ilinted the ,diginal con- ception of the design t r assassinate the king, The pill a middish witch-craft is over us from the opening of , tt.e play, and the prophetic utterances of the Weird Sisters stir the unnatural dedres which already</p>
